1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,525
Chapter 4: Maternal Physiology.

3
00:00:05,525 --> 00:00:25,500
Reproductive Tract Breasts Skin Metabolic Changes Hematological Changes Cardiovascular System Respiratory Tract Urinary System Gastrointestinal Tract Endocrine System Musculoskeletal System Central Nervous System References The anatomical, physiological, and biochemical adaptations of pregnancy are profound.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:31,612
Many gestational changes begin soon after fertilization and continue throughout pregnancy.

5
00:00:31,612 --> 00:00:38,300
Equally astounding is the almost complete return to the prepregnancy state after delivery and lactation.

6
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:44,625
Most pregnancyrelated changes are prompted by stimuli provided by the fetus and placenta.

7
00:00:44,625 --> 00:00:53,462
Virtually every organ system undergoes alterations, and these can appreciably modify criteria for disease diagnosis and treatment.

8
00:00:53,462 --> 00:00:59,637
Thus, an understanding of pregnancy adaptations is essential to avoid misinterpretation.

9
00:00:59,637 --> 00:01:06,087
Moreover, some normal physiological changes can unmask or worsen preexisting disease.

10
00:01:06,087 --> 00:01:08,275
Reproductive Tract.

11
00:01:08,275 --> 00:01:10,425
# Uterus.

12
00:01:10,425 --> 00:01:19,575
In the nonpregnant woman, the uterus weighs approximately 70 g and is almost solid, except for a cavity of 10 mL or less.

13
00:01:19,575 --> 00:01:29,975
During pregnancy, the uterus is transformed into a thin-walled muscular organ of sufficient capacity to accommodate the fetus, placenta, and amnionic fluid.

14
00:01:29,975 --> 00:01:35,900
The total volume of the contents at term averages 5 L but may be 20 L or more!

15
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:44,787
Thus, by the end of pregnancy, the uterus has achieved a capacity that is 500 to 1000 times greater than the nonpregnant state.

16
00:01:44,787 --> 00:01:58,612
The corresponding increase in uterine weight is such that, by term, the organ weighs nearly 1100 g. Uterine hypertrophy early in pregnancy is probably stimulated by the action of estrogen and perhaps progesterone.

17
00:01:58,612 --> 00:02:03,600
Thus, similar uterine changes can be observed with ectopic pregnancy.

18
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:12,750
But after approximately 12 weeks’ gestation, uterine growth is related predominantly to pressure exerted by the expanding products of conception.

19
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:17,025
Within the uterus, enlargement is most marked in the fundus.

20
00:02:17,025 --> 00:02:22,625
The extent of uterine hypertrophy is also influenced by the position of the placenta.

21
00:02:22,625 --> 00:02:28,912
Namely, the myometrium surrounding the placental site grows more rapidly than does the rest.

22
00:02:28,912 --> 00:02:38,137
During pregnancy, uterine enlargement involves stretching and marked hypertrophy of muscle cells, whereas the production of new myocytes is limited.

23
00:02:38,137 --> 00:02:47,150
Fibrous tissue also accumulates, particularly in the external muscle layer, together with a considerable rise in elastic tissue content.

24
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:54,300
The walls of the corpus considerably thicken and strengthen during the first few months of pregnancy but then gradually thin.

25
00:02:54,300 --> 00:03:04,112
By term, the myometrium is only 1 to 2 cm thick, and the fetus usually can be palpated through the soft, readily indentable uterine walls.

26
00:03:04,112 --> 00:03:08,687
The uterine musculature during pregnancy is arranged in three strata.

27
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:15,337
The first is an outer hoodlike layer, which arches over the fundus and extends into the various ligaments.

28
00:03:15,337 --> 00:03:21,650
The middle layer is a dense network of muscle fibers perforated in all directions by blood vessels.

29
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:29,450
Last is an internal layer, with sphincter-like fibers around the fallopian tube orifices and internal cervical os.

30
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:33,137
Most of the uterine wall is formed by the middle layer.

31
00:03:33,137 --> 00:03:39,850
Here, each myocyte has a double curve so that the interlacing of any two cells forms a figure eight.

32
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:47,662
This crucial arrangement permits myocytes to contract after delivery and constrict penetrating blood vessels to halt bleeding.

33
00:03:47,662 --> 00:03:54,750
Uterine Shape and Position For the first few weeks, the uterus maintains its original piriform or pear shape.

34
00:03:54,750 --> 00:04:02,550
But, as pregnancy advances, the corpus and fundus become globular and almost spherical by 12 weeks’ gestation.

35
00:04:02,550 --> 00:04:08,462
Subsequently, the organ grows more rapidly in length than in width and becomes ovoid.

36
00:04:08,462 --> 00:04:13,525
By the end of 12 weeks, the enlarged uterus extends out of the pelvis.

37
00:04:13,525 --> 00:04:23,362
With growth, it contacts the anterior abdominal wall, displaces the intestines laterally and superiorly, and ultimately reaches almost to the liver.

38
00:04:23,362 --> 00:04:32,850
As it ascends, the uterus usually rotates to the right, and this dextrorotation likely is caused by the rectosigmoid on the left side of the pelvis.

39
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:36,575
As the uterus rises, tension is exerted on the

40
00:04:36,724 --> 00:04:39,061
broad and round ligaments.

41
00:04:39,011 --> 00:04:47,111
With the pregnant woman standing, the longitudinal axis of the uterus corresponds to an extension of the pelvic inlet axis.

42
00:04:47,111 --> 00:04:53,311
The abdominal wall supports the uterus and maintains this axis, unless the wall is lax.

43
00:04:53,311 --> 00:05:00,799
When the pregnant woman lies supine, the uterus falls back to rest on the vertebral column and the adjacent great vessels.

44
00:05:00,799 --> 00:05:09,536
Uterine Contractility Beginning in early pregnancy, the uterus contracts irregularly, and these may be perceived as mild cramps.

45
00:05:09,536 --> 00:05:15,724
During the second trimester, these contractions can be detected by bimanual examination.

46
00:05:15,724 --> 00:05:23,361
In 1872, J. Braxton Hicks first brought attention to these contractions, which now bear his name.

47
00:05:23,361 --> 00:05:26,811
These appear unpredictably and sporadically.

48
00:05:26,811 --> 00:05:31,874
Their intensity varies between 5 and 25 mm Hg .

49
00:05:31,874 --> 00:05:39,136
Until near term, these Braxton Hicks contractions are infrequent, but their number rises during the last week or two.

50
00:05:39,136 --> 00:05:46,249
At this time, the uterus may contract as often as every 10 to 20 minutes and with some degree of rhythmicity.

51
00:05:46,249 --> 00:05:56,286
Correspondingly, uterine electrical activity is low and uncoordinated early in gestation but becomes progressively more intense and synchronized by term .

52
00:05:56,286 --> 00:06:01,399
This synchrony develops twice as fast in multiparas compared with nulliparas .

53
00:06:01,399 --> 00:06:10,424
Late in pregnancy, these contractions may cause some discomfort and account for so-called false labor (Chap 21, p. 401).

54
00:06:10,424 --> 00:06:22,999
Uteroplacental Blood Flow The delivery of most substances essential for fetal and placental growth, metabolism, and waste removal requires the placental intervillous space to be adequately perfused .

55
00:06:22,999 --> 00:06:36,224
Placental perfusion depends on total uterine blood flow, but simultaneous measurement of uterine, ovarian, and collateral vessels is not yet possible, even using magnetic resonance (MR) angiography .

56
00:06:36,224 --> 00:06:43,036
For this measurement, early results using fourdimensional flow MR imaging in the primate are promising .

57
00:06:43,036 --> 00:06:51,211
Doppler ultrasound interrogation of the uterine arteries shows a progressive increase in uteroplacental blood flow during pregnancy.

58
00:06:51,211 --> 00:07:06,874
Flow rates rise from approximately 450 mL/min in the midtrimester to nearly 500 to 750 mL/min at 36 weeks’ gestation (Flo, 2014; Wilson, 2007).

59
00:07:06,874 --> 00:07:18,986
These measures are similar to flow estimates ascertained indirectly using clearance rates of androstenedione and xenon-133 (Edman, 1981; Kauppila, 1980).

60
00:07:18,986 --> 00:07:31,449
These values also mirror older ones—500 to 750 mL/min—obtained with invasive methods (Assali, 1953; Metcalfe, 1955).

61
00:07:31,449 --> 00:07:39,049
Logically, such massively increased uteroplacental blood flow requires adaptation of the uterine veins as well.

62
00:07:39,049 --> 00:07:46,449
The resulting increased venous caliber and distensibility can result in uterine vein varices that rarely may rupture .

63
00:07:46,449 --> 00:07:56,499
As noted first from animal studies, uterine contractions, either spontaneous or induced, lower uterine blood flow proportionally to contraction intensity .

64
00:07:56,499 --> 00:08:01,361
A tetanic contraction yields a precipitous fall in uterine blood flow.

65
00:08:01,361 --> 00:08:09,574
In humans, three-dimensional power Doppler angiography has also demonstrated reduced uterine blood flow during contractions .

66
00:08:09,574 --> 00:08:19,299
Using a similar technique, resistance to blood flow in both maternal and fetal vessels was found to be greater during the second stage of labor compared with the first .

67
00:08:19,299 --> 00:08:29,836
Given that baseline uterine blood flow is diminished in pregnancies complicated by fetal-growth restriction, these fetuses often tolerate spontaneous labor less effectively .

68
00:08:29,836 --> 00:08:38,399
Uteroplacental Blood Flow Regulation The vessels that supply the uterine corpus widen and elongate yet preserve their contractile function .

69
00:08:38,399 --> 00:08:46,486
As an exception, the spiral arteries, which directly supply the placenta, vasodilate but completely lose contractility.

70
00:08:46,486 --> 00:08:54,061
This presumably stems from endovascular trophoblast invasion, which destroys the intramural muscular elements .

71
00:08:54,061 --> 00:09:00,524
This vasodilation allows maternal–placental blood flow to progressively rise during gestation.

72
00:09:00,524 --> 00:09:10,874
Given that blood flow increases proportionally to the fourth power of the radius of the vessel, small widening of a vessel diameter results in tremendous augmentation of flow.

73
00:09:10,874 --> 00:09:20,836
In one study, the uterine artery diameter grew from only 3.3 mm to 3.7 mm between 22 and 29 weeks’

74
00:09:21,004 --> 00:09:28,916
gestation, but mean blood flow velocity increased 50 percent, from 29 to 43 cm/sec .

75
00:09:28,866 --> 00:09:36,654
The downstream fall in vascular resistance is another key factor that accelerates flow and shear stress in upstream vessels.

76
00:09:36,654 --> 00:09:41,166
In turn, shear stress leads to circumferential vessel growth.

77
00:09:41,166 --> 00:09:50,641
Nitric oxide—a potent vasodilator— appears to play a central role in regulating this process and is discussed later (p. 65).

78
00:09:50,641 --> 00:10:04,866
Endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) and nitric oxide production are augmented by endothelial shear stress and several hormones and growth factors (Osol, 2019; Zhang, 2017).

79
00:10:04,866 --> 00:10:17,079
Factors include estrogen, progesterone, activin, placental growth factor (PlGF), and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), which is a promoter of angiogenesis.

80
00:10:17,079 --> 00:10:30,341
As an important aside, VEGF and PlGF signaling is attenuated in response to excess placental secretion of their soluble receptor—soluble FMS-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1).

81
00:10:30,341 --> 00:10:41,116
An elevated maternal sFlt-1 level inactivates and lowers circulating PlGF and VEGF concentrations and is important in preeclampsia pathogenesis .

82
00:10:41,116 --> 00:10:51,504
Normal pregnancy is also characterized by vascular refractoriness to the pressor effects of infused angiotensin II, and this raises uteroplacental blood flow .

83
00:10:51,504 --> 00:10:58,241
Other factors that augment uteroplacental blood flow include relaxin and certain adipocytokines .

84
00:10:58,241 --> 00:11:04,129
Chemerin is an adipocytokine secreted by several tissues, including the placenta .

85
00:11:04,129 --> 00:11:11,166
Its concentration rises as gestation advances and serves to increase human umbilical eNOS activity .

86
00:11:11,166 --> 00:11:21,016
Another adipocytokine—visfatin—raises VEGF secretion and VEGF receptor 2 expression in epithelial cells derived from the placental amnion .

87
00:11:21,016 --> 00:11:32,204
Other adipocytokines include leptin, resistin, and adiponectin, which all enhance umbilical vein endothelial cell proliferation (Połeć, 2014).

88
00:11:32,204 --> 00:11:39,741
Last, certain microRNA species mediate vascular remodeling and uterine blood flow early in placentation .

89
00:11:39,741 --> 00:11:48,791
In particular, members of the miR-17–92 cluster and miR-34 are important in spiral artery remodeling and invasion.

90
00:11:48,791 --> 00:11:56,391
Micro-RNA dysfunction has been reported in preeclampsia, fetalgrowth restriction, and gestational diabetes.

91
00:11:56,391 --> 00:11:58,529
# Cervix.

92
00:11:58,529 --> 00:12:04,866
As early as 1 month after conception, the cervix begins to soften and gain bluish tones.

93
00:12:04,866 --> 00:12:16,629
These changes result from increased vascularity and edema of the entire cervix, from alterations in the collagen network, and from hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the cervical glands .

94
00:12:16,629 --> 00:12:23,191
Although the cervix contains a small amount of smooth muscle, its major component is connective tissue.

95
00:12:23,191 --> 00:12:36,779
Rearrangement of this collagen-rich tissue aids the cervix in retention of the pregnancy until term, in dilation to aid delivery, and in postpartum repair and reconstitution to permit a subsequent successful pregnancy .

96
00:12:36,779 --> 00:12:50,879
As detailed in Chapter 21 (p. 405), cervical ripening involves connective tissue remodeling that lowers collagen and proteoglycan concentrations and raises water content compared with the nonpregnant cervix.

97
00:12:50,879 --> 00:12:59,629
Cervical glands undergo marked proliferation, and by the end of pregnancy, they occupy up to one half of the entire cervical mass.

98
00:12:59,629 --> 00:13:09,541
This normal pregnancy-induced change prompts an extension, or eversion, of the proliferating columnar endocervical glands onto the ectocervical portio .

99
00:13:09,541 --> 00:13:16,179
This tissue appears red and velvety and bleeds even with minor trauma, such as with Pap smear testing.

100
00:13:16,179 --> 00:13:18,541
Figure 4-1.

101
00:13:18,541 --> 00:13:22,641
Cervical eversion of pregnancy as viewed through a colposcope.

102
00:13:22,641 --> 00:13:27,741
The eversion represents columnar epithelium on the portio of the cervix.

103
00:13:27,741 --> 00:13:31,516
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Claudia Werner.

104
00:13:31,516 --> 00:13:39,979
The endocervical mucosal cells produce copious amounts of tenacious mucus that obstructs the cervical canal soon after conception .

105
00:13:39,979 --> 00:13:49,191
This mucus is rich in immunoglobulins and cytokines and may act as an immunological barrier to protect the uterine contents against infection .

106
00:13:49,191 --> 00:13:55,579
At labor onset, if not before, this mucus plug is expelled, resulting in a bloody show.

107
00:13:55,579 --> 00:14:00,579
Moreover, the cervical mucus consistency changes during pregnancy.

108
00:14:00,579 --> 00:14:08,966
Specifically, in most pregnant women, as a result of progesterone, when cervical mucus is spread and dried on a glass slide, it

109
00:14:09,124 --> 00:14:12,661
shows poor crystallization, termed beading.

110
00:14:12,611 --> 00:14:21,899
In some gravidas, as a result of amnionic fluid leakage, an arborization of ice-like crystals, called ferning, is seen microscopically.

111
00:14:21,899 --> 00:14:30,599
Histologically, basal cells near the squamocolumnar junction can be prominent in size, shape, and staining quality in pregnancy.

112
00:14:30,599 --> 00:14:34,386
These changes are considered to be estrogen induced.

113
00:14:34,386 --> 00:14:43,474
In addition, pregnancy is associated with both endocervical gland hyperplasia and hypersecretory appearance— the Arias-Stella reaction.

114
00:14:43,474 --> 00:14:52,324
This cytologic change can make differentiating these from truly atypical glandular cells during Pap test evaluation particularly difficult .

115
00:14:52,324 --> 00:14:54,486
# Ovaries.

116
00:14:54,486 --> 00:15:00,399
Ovulation ceases during pregnancy, and maturation of new follicles is suspended.

117
00:15:00,399 --> 00:15:08,924
The single corpus luteum functions maximally during the first 6 to 7 weeks of pregnancy—4 to 5 weeks postovulation.

118
00:15:08,924 --> 00:15:13,836
Thereafter, it contributes relatively little to progesterone production.

119
00:15:13,836 --> 00:15:27,299
As discussed in Chapter 66 (p. 1170), surgical removal of the corpus luteum before 7 weeks’ gestation prompts a rapid fall in maternal serum progesterone levels and spontaneous abortion .

120
00:15:27,299 --> 00:15:35,024
In these cases, exogenous progesterone is necessary for pregnancy maintenance until placental function is sufficient.

121
00:15:35,024 --> 00:15:41,336
After 7 weeks, however, corpus luteum excision ordinarily does not cause abortion.

122
00:15:41,336 --> 00:15:50,436
An extrauterine decidual reaction on and just beneath the ovarian surface is common in pregnancy and is usually observed at cesarean delivery.

123
00:15:50,436 --> 00:16:00,124
These appear as slightly elevated clear or red blisters or patches that bleed easily and may, on first glance, resemble freshly torn adhesions.

124
00:16:00,124 --> 00:16:08,324
Similar decidual reactions are seen on the uterine serosa and other pelvic, or even extrapelvic, abdominal organs .

125
00:16:08,324 --> 00:16:15,874
These areas arise from subcoelomic mesenchyme or endometriotic lesions that have been stimulated by progesterone.

126
00:16:15,874 --> 00:16:22,211
They histologically appear similar to progestin-stimulated intrauterine endometrial stroma .

127
00:16:22,211 --> 00:16:27,799
The enormous caliber of the ovarian veins viewed at cesarean delivery is startling.

128
00:16:27,799 --> 00:16:40,399
Hodgkinson (1953) found that the diameter of the ovarian vascular pedicle increased during pregnancy from 0.9 cm to approximately 2.6 cm at term.

129
00:16:40,399 --> 00:16:47,236
Again, recall that flow in a tubular structure increases exponentially as the diameter enlarges.

130
00:16:47,236 --> 00:16:58,049
Relaxin This protein hormone is secreted by the corpus luteum, the decidua, and the placenta in a pattern similar to that of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) .

131
00:16:58,049 --> 00:17:02,486
Relaxin is also expressed in brain, heart, and kidney.

132
00:17:02,486 --> 00:17:14,336
It is mentioned here because its secretion by the corpus luteum appears to aid many maternal physiological adaptations, such as remodeling of reproductive-tract connective tissue to accommodate labor .

133
00:17:14,336 --> 00:17:26,949
Relaxin also appears important in initiating augmented renal hemodynamics, lowering serum osmolality, and increasing arterial compliance, which are all associated with normal pregnancy .

134
00:17:26,949 --> 00:17:39,074
Despite its name, serum relaxin levels do not contribute to greater peripheral joint laxity or pelvic girdle pain during pregnancy (Aldabe, 2012; Marnach, 2003).

135
00:17:39,074 --> 00:17:48,811
Theca-Lutein Cysts These benign ovarian lesions reflect exaggerated physiological follicle stimulation, which is termed hyperreactio luteinalis.

136
00:17:48,811 --> 00:17:53,436
The resulting bilateral cystic ovaries are variably enlarged.

137
00:17:53,436 --> 00:17:58,874
The reaction is usually linked to markedly elevated serum hCG levels.

138
00:17:58,874 --> 00:18:08,349
Logically, theca-lutein cysts are found frequently with gestational trophoblastic disease (Fig. 13-3, p. 238).

139
00:18:08,349 --> 00:18:16,836
They may also develop with the placentomegaly that can accompany diabetes, antiD alloimmunization, and multifetal gestation.

140
00:18:16,836 --> 00:18:31,674
Hyperreactio luteinalis is associated with preeclampsia and hyperthyroidism, which may contribute to elevated risks for fetal-growth restriction and preterm birth (Lynn, 2013; Malinowski, 2015).

141
00:18:31,674 --> 00:18:37,199
These cysts are also encountered in women with otherwise uncomplicated pregnancies.

142
00:18:37,199 --> 00:18:47,674
In these cases, an exaggerated response of the ovaries to normal levels of circulating hCG is suspected (Sarmento Gonçalves, 2015).

143
00:18:47,674 --> 00:18:53,649
Although usually asymptomatic, hemorrhage into the cysts can cause acute abdominal pain .

144
00:18:53,649 --> 00:18:57,874
Maternal virilization may be seen in up to 30 percent of

145
00:18:58,036 --> 00:19:03,298
women, however, virilization of the fetus has only rarely been reported.

146
00:19:03,248 --> 00:19:13,673
Maternal findings that include temporal balding, hirsutism, and clitoromegaly are associated with massively elevated levels of androstenedione and testosterone.

147
00:19:13,673 --> 00:19:22,873
The diagnosis typically is based on sonographic findings of bilateral enlarged ovaries containing multiple cysts in the appropriate clinical settings.

148
00:19:22,873 --> 00:19:27,061
The condition is self-limited and resolves following delivery.

149
00:19:27,061 --> 00:19:35,998
Its management was reviewed by Malinowski (2015) and discussed further in Chapter 66 (p. 1171).

150
00:19:35,998 --> 00:19:38,611
# Fallopian Tubes.

151
00:19:38,611 --> 00:19:45,698
The fallopian tube musculature, that is, the myosalpinx, undergoes little hypertrophy during pregnancy.

152
00:19:45,698 --> 00:19:49,623
The epithelium of the endosalpinx somewhat flattens.

153
00:19:49,623 --> 00:19:57,086
Decidual cells may develop in the stroma of the endosalpinx, but a continuous decidual membrane is not formed.

154
00:19:57,086 --> 00:20:10,061
Rarely, a fallopian tube may twist during uterine enlargement, but this torsion is more common with comorbid paratubal or ovarian cysts (Lee, 2015; Macedo, 2017).

155
00:20:10,061 --> 00:20:12,911
# Vagina and Perineum.

156
00:20:12,911 --> 00:20:23,498
During pregnancy, greater vascularity and hyperemia develop in the skin and muscles of the perineum and vulva, and the underlying abundant connective tissue softens.

157
00:20:23,498 --> 00:20:32,161
This augmented vascularity prominently affects the vagina and cervix and results in the violet color characteristic of the Chadwick sign.

158
00:20:32,161 --> 00:20:40,961
Within the vagina, the considerably elevated volume of cervical secretions during pregnancy forms a somewhat thick, white discharge.

159
00:20:40,961 --> 00:20:45,248
The pH is acidic and varies from 3.5 to 6.

160
00:20:45,248 --> 00:20:55,523
This pH results from increased production of lactic acid by Lactobacillus acidophilus during metabolism of glycogen energy stores in the vaginal epithelium.

161
00:20:55,523 --> 00:21:04,261
Pregnancy is associated with an elevated risk of vulvovaginal candidiasis, particularly during the second and third trimesters.

162
00:21:04,261 --> 00:21:11,498
Higher infection rates may stem from immunological and hormonal changes and from greater vaginal glycogen stores .

163
00:21:11,498 --> 00:21:18,523
The vaginal walls undergo striking changes in preparation for the distention that accompanies labor and delivery.

164
00:21:18,523 --> 00:21:26,148
These include considerable epithelial thickening, connective tissue loosening, and hypertrophy of smooth muscle cells.

165
00:21:26,148 --> 00:21:36,348
Pelvic Organ Prolapse Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) scores and sonographic studies show that vaginal support changes across pregnancy.

166
00:21:36,348 --> 00:21:52,298
In particular, vaginal lengthening, posterior vaginal wall and hiatal relaxation, increased levator hiatal area, and greater first-trimester vaginal elastase activity are all associated with uncomplicated spontaneous vaginal delivery .

167
00:21:52,298 --> 00:22:01,073
The larger hiatal area persists in women who deliver vaginally compared with women delivering by prelabor or early-labor cesarean delivery.

168
00:22:01,073 --> 00:22:13,873
However, all women show greater hiatal distensibility after delivery, which is potentially a factor in later pelvic floor dysfunction (Blomqvist, 2018; Friedman, 2019).

169
00:22:13,873 --> 00:22:24,461
In women with existing apical vaginal prolapse, the cervix, and occasionally a portion of the uterine body, can protrude variably from the vulva during early pregnancy.

170
00:22:24,461 --> 00:22:30,848
With further growth, the uterus usually rises above the pelvis and can draw the cervix up with it.

171
00:22:30,848 --> 00:22:39,073
If the uterus persists in its prolapsed position, symptoms of incarceration may develop at 10 to 14 weeks’ gestation .

172
00:22:39,073 --> 00:22:46,436
As a preventive measure, the uterus can be replaced early in pregnancy and held in position with a space-filling pessary.

173
00:22:46,436 --> 00:22:53,298
Attenuation of anterior vaginal wall support can lead to prolapse of the bladder, that is, cystocele.

174
00:22:53,298 --> 00:22:57,698
Associated urinary stasis can predispose to infection.

175
00:22:57,698 --> 00:23:09,836
Pregnancy may also worsen coexistent stress urinary incontinence (SUI), likely because urethral closing pressures do not rise sufficiently to compensate for altered bladder neck support.

176
00:23:09,836 --> 00:23:18,361
Urinary incontinence affects nearly 20 percent of women during the first trimester and nearly 40 percent during the third trimester.

177
00:23:18,361 --> 00:23:27,898
Most cases stem from SUI rather than urgency urinary incontinence (Abdullah, 2016; Franco, 2014).

178
00:23:27,898 --> 00:23:40,148
In primigravidas, maternal age greater than 30 years, obesity, smoking, constipation, and gestational diabetes mellitus are all risk factors associated with incontinence during

179
00:23:40,300 --> 00:23:42,025
pregnancy .

180
00:23:41,975 --> 00:23:49,562
Attenuation of posterior vaginal wall support can lead to rectocele, and a large defect may fill with feces.

181
00:23:49,562 --> 00:23:57,112
Splinting, in which a finger is inserted into the vagina, can bolster posterior wall support to aid evacuation.

182
00:23:57,112 --> 00:24:01,150
Occasionally, digital rectal disimpaction is needed.

183
00:24:01,150 --> 00:24:06,887
Uncommonly during labor, fetal descent is blocked by a cystocele or rectocele.

184
00:24:06,887 --> 00:24:13,900
Emptying by catheterization or enema and compressing the bulge with vaginal fingers will resolve the impasse.

185
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:18,837
Rarely, a large enterocele may bulge into the vagina to block descent.

186
00:24:18,837 --> 00:24:24,137
Similarly, the hernia sac and its abdominal contents are gently reduced.

187
00:24:24,137 --> 00:24:25,675
Breasts.

188
00:24:25,675 --> 00:24:31,325
In early pregnancy, women often experience breast tenderness and paresthesias.

189
00:24:31,325 --> 00:24:37,950
After the second month, the breasts grow in size, and delicate veins are visible just beneath the skin.

190
00:24:37,950 --> 00:24:43,487
The nipples become considerably larger, more deeply pigmented, and more erectile.

191
00:24:43,487 --> 00:24:51,600
After the first few months, a thick, yellowish fluid—colostrum—can often be expressed from the nipples by gentle massage.

192
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:56,800
During the same months, the areolae become broader and more deeply pigmented.

193
00:24:56,800 --> 00:25:04,950
Scattered through each areola are several small elevations, the glands of Montgomery, which are hypertrophic sebaceous glands.

194
00:25:04,950 --> 00:25:11,562
If breasts gain extensive size, skin striae similar to those observed in the abdomen may develop.

195
00:25:11,562 --> 00:25:26,800
Rarely, breasts can become pathologically enlarged—referred to as gigantomastia—which may require consideration of postpartum bromocriptine and later surgical reduction (Rezai, 2015; Türkan, 2016).

196
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:33,000
For most, prepregnancy breast size and ultimate volume of breast milk do not correlate.

197
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:39,800
Multiple factors influence milk production and are discussed in Chapter 36 (p. 640).

198
00:25:39,800 --> 00:25:41,337
Skin.

199
00:25:41,337 --> 00:25:50,600
Skin changes are common, and Fernandes and Amaral (2015) described dermatological changes in more than 900 pregnant women.

200
00:25:50,600 --> 00:25:56,912
They found at least one physiological cutaneous change in 89 percent of the women examined.

201
00:25:56,912 --> 00:26:02,212
Dermatological pathologies during pregnancy are found in Chapter 65.

202
00:26:02,212 --> 00:26:04,637
# Abdominal Wall.

203
00:26:04,637 --> 00:26:10,712
Beginning after midpregnancy, reddish, slightly depressed streaks commonly develop in the skin.

204
00:26:10,712 --> 00:26:14,437
These are called striae gravidarum or stretch marks.

205
00:26:14,437 --> 00:26:22,062
In multiparas, glistening, silvery lines that represent the cicatrices of previous striae frequently coexist.

206
00:26:22,062 --> 00:26:33,400
In one study of 800 primiparas, 70 percent developed striae gravidarum on their abdomen; 33 percent on their breasts; and 41 percent on their hips and thighs .

207
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:42,562
The strongest associated risk factors included younger maternal age, family history, and prepregnancy weight and weight gain during pregnancy.

208
00:26:42,562 --> 00:26:51,437
The etiology of striae gravidarum is unknown, but aloe vera gel and almond oil decrease itching and may help to prevent progression .

209
00:26:51,437 --> 00:26:57,950
Occasionally, the muscles of the abdominal walls do not withstand the tension of the expanding pregnancy.

210
00:26:57,950 --> 00:27:04,950
As a result, rectus muscles separate in the midline, creating diastasis recti of varying extent.

211
00:27:04,950 --> 00:27:15,662
If severe, a considerable portion of the anterior uterine wall is covered by only a layer of skin, attenuated fascia, and peritoneum to form a ventral hernia.

212
00:27:15,662 --> 00:27:18,350
# Hyperpigmentation.

213
00:27:18,350 --> 00:27:25,237
This develops in up to 90 percent of women and is usually more accentuated in those with darker complexion .

214
00:27:25,237 --> 00:27:36,312
Of specific sites, the pigmented skin line in the midline of the anterior abdominal wall—the linea alba—takes on dark brown-black pigmentation to form the linea nigra.

215
00:27:36,312 --> 00:27:46,750
Occasionally, irregular brownish patches of varying size appear on the face and neck, giving rise to chloasma or melasma gravidarum—the mask of pregnancy.

216
00:27:46,750 --> 00:27:51,687
Pigmentation of the areolae and genital skin also may be accentuated.

217
00:27:51,687 --> 00:27:58,162
After delivery, these pigmentary changes usually disappear or at least regress considerably.

218
00:27:58,162 --> 00:28:02,125
Oral contraceptives may cause similar alterations .

219
00:28:02,125 --> 00:28:10,487
The etiology of these pigmentary changes is incompletely understood, however, hormonal and genetic factors are implicated.

220
00:28:10,487 --> 00:28:24,612
Levels of melanocytestimulating hormone, a polypeptide similar to corticotropin, are elevated remarkably throughout pregnancy, and estrogen and progesterone also are reported to have melanocyte-stimulating effects.

221
00:28:24,612 --> 00:28:26,787
# Vascular

222
00:28:26,956 --> 00:28:28,643
Changes.

223
00:28:28,593 --> 00:28:36,106
Angiomas, called vascular spiders, are particularly common on the face, neck, upper chest, and arms.

224
00:28:36,106 --> 00:28:41,706
These are minute, red skin papules with radicles branching out from a central lesion.

225
00:28:41,706 --> 00:28:47,493
The condition is often designated as nevus, angioma, or telangiectasis.

226
00:28:47,493 --> 00:28:51,181
Palmar erythema is encountered during pregnancy.

227
00:28:51,181 --> 00:28:57,581
Both conditions lack clinical significance and disappear in most gravidas shortly after pregnancy.

228
00:28:57,581 --> 00:29:01,393
They are likely the consequence of hyperestrogenemia.

229
00:29:01,393 --> 00:29:10,856
In addition to these discrete lesions, increased cutaneous blood flow in pregnancy serves to dissipate excess heat generated by augmented metabolism.

230
00:29:10,856 --> 00:29:13,268
# Hair Changes.

231
00:29:13,268 --> 00:29:27,381
Throughout life, the human hair follicle undergoes a pattern of cyclic activity that includes periods of hair growth (anagen phase), apoptosis-driven involution (catagen phase), and a resting period (telogen phase).

232
00:29:27,381 --> 00:29:36,931
Based on a study of 116 healthy pregnant women, the anagen phase lengthens during pregnancy and the telogen rate increases postpartum .

233
00:29:36,931 --> 00:29:44,506
Neither is exaggerated in most gravidas, but excessive hair loss in the puerperium is termed telogen effluvium .

234
00:29:44,506 --> 00:29:46,831
Metabolic Changes.

235
00:29:46,831 --> 00:29:55,393
In response to the greater demands of the rapidly growing fetus and placenta, the pregnant woman undergoes numerous metabolic changes.

236
00:29:55,393 --> 00:30:03,031
By the third trimester, maternal basal metabolic rate rises by 20 percent compared with that of the nonpregnant state .

237
00:30:03,031 --> 00:30:08,068
This rate grows by an additional 10 percent in women with a twin gestation .

238
00:30:08,068 --> 00:30:19,118
This is stratified as 85, 285, and 475 kcal/d during the first, second, and third trimesters, respectively .

239
00:30:19,118 --> 00:30:31,981
Of note, Abeysekera and coworkers (2016) reported that women accrue fat mass during pregnancy despite the increased total energy expenditure and without significant change in energy intake.

240
00:30:31,981 --> 00:30:35,431
This suggests more efficient energy storage.

241
00:30:35,431 --> 00:30:37,693
Table 4-1.

242
00:30:37,693 --> 00:30:51,131
Additional Energy Demands During Normal Pregnancya Most of the normal weight gain in pregnancy is attributable to the uterus and its contents, the breasts, and expanded blood and extravascular extracellular fluid volumes.

243
00:30:51,131 --> 00:31:01,181
A smaller fraction results from metabolic alterations that promote accumulation of cellular water, fat, and protein, which are so-called maternal reserves.

244
00:31:01,181 --> 00:31:16,493
The average weight gain during pregnancy approximates 12.5 kg or 27.5 lb, and this value has remained consistent across studies and over time (Hytten, 1991; Jebeile, 2016).

245
00:31:16,493 --> 00:31:22,493
Weight gain is detailed in Table 4-2 and in Chapter 10 (p. 183) .

246
00:31:22,493 --> 00:31:24,793
Table 4-2.

247
00:31:24,793 --> 00:31:30,031
Weight Gain Based on Pregnancy-Related Components # Water Metabolism.

248
00:31:30,031 --> 00:31:38,731
In pregnancy, greater water retention is normal and mediated in part by a drop in plasma osmolality of 10 mOsm/kg.

249
00:31:38,731 --> 00:31:48,906
As shown in Figure 4-2, this decline develops in early pregnancy and is induced by a reset of osmotic thresholds for thirst and vasopressin secretion .

250
00:31:48,906 --> 00:31:52,543
Relaxin and other hormones are thought to play a role .

251
00:31:52,543 --> 00:31:54,831
Figure 4-2.

252
00:31:54,831 --> 00:32:07,768
Mean values (black line) ± standard deviations (blue lines) for plasma osmolality (P osm) measured at weekly intervals in nine women from preconception to 16 weeks.

253
00:32:07,768 --> 00:32:13,181
LMP = last menstrual period; MP = menstrual period.

254
00:32:13,181 --> 00:32:26,368
Redrawn from Davison JM, Dunlop W. Renal hemodynamics and tubular function normal human pregnancy, Kidney Int 1980 Aug;18(2):152–161.

255
00:32:26,368 --> 00:32:34,756
Maternal serum osmolality is significantly lower than umbilical arterial osmolality to favor water transport to the fetus .

256
00:32:34,756 --> 00:32:42,231
At term, the water content of the fetus, placenta, and amnionic fluid approximates 3.5 L.

257
00:32:42,231 --> 00:32:49,243
Another 3.0 L accumulates from expanded maternal blood volume and from uterus and breast growth.

258
00:32:49,243 --> 00:32:57,193
Thus, the minimum amount of extra water that the average woman accrues during normal pregnancy approximates 6.5 L.

259
00:32:57,193 --> 00:33:06,418
This corresponds to almost 15 lb. Demonstrable pitting edema of the ankles and legs is seen in most gravidas, especially at the day’s end.

260
00:33:06,418 --> 00:33:10,318
This fluid accumulation may amount to a liter or so.

261
00:33:10,318 --> 00:33:17,393
It results from greater venous pressure below the level of the uterus as a consequence of partial vena cava occlusion.

262
00:33:17,393 --> 00:33:26,918
A decline in interstitial colloid osmotic pressure induced by normal pregnancy also favors edema late in pregnancy (Øian, 1985).

263
00:33:26,918 --> 00:33:29,668
Longitudinal studies of body

264
00:33:29,836 --> 00:33:36,123
composition show a progressive accumulation of total body water and fat mass during pregnancy.

265
00:33:36,073 --> 00:33:44,048
These two components as well as initial maternal weight and weight gained during pregnancy are highly associated with neonatal birthweight .

266
00:33:44,048 --> 00:33:53,948
As discussed in Chapter 51 (p. 905), “overnourished” women are more likely to deliver oversized neonates, even when glucose tolerant.

267
00:33:53,948 --> 00:33:56,698
# Protein Metabolism.

268
00:33:56,698 --> 00:34:04,673
The products of conception, the uterus, and maternal blood are relatively rich in protein rather than fat or carbohydrate.

269
00:34:04,673 --> 00:34:15,948
At term, the normally grown fetus and placenta together weigh about 4 kg and contain approximately 500 g of protein, or about half of the total pregnancy increase.

270
00:34:15,948 --> 00:34:27,023
The remaining 500 g is added to the uterus as contractile protein, to the breasts primarily in the glands, and to maternal blood as hemoglobin and plasma proteins.

271
00:34:27,023 --> 00:34:36,023
Amino acid concentrations are higher in the fetal than in the maternal compartment and generally result from facilitated transport across the placenta .

272
00:34:36,023 --> 00:34:42,823
This greater concentration is largely regulated by the placenta through an incompletely understood process .

273
00:34:42,823 --> 00:34:49,411
In particular, placental transport is variable for individuals and for different amino acids.

274
00:34:49,411 --> 00:34:56,536
For example, tyrosine is a conditionally essential amino acid in the preterm neonate but not in the fetus .

275
00:34:56,536 --> 00:35:08,023
The placenta concentrates amino acids into the fetal circulation and is also involved in protein synthesis, oxidation, and transamination of some nonessential amino acids .

276
00:35:08,023 --> 00:35:14,536
Maternal protein intake does not appear to be a critical determinant for birthweight among well-nourished women .

277
00:35:14,536 --> 00:35:20,886
Still, recent data suggest that current recommendations for protein intake may be too low.

278
00:35:20,886 --> 00:35:27,048
These guidelines are extrapolated from nonpregnant adults and may underestimate actual needs.

279
00:35:27,048 --> 00:35:34,486
Stephens and colleagues (2015) prospectively analyzed maternal protein intake and metabolism.

280
00:35:34,486 --> 00:35:46,661
They estimated average requirements of 1.22 g/kg/d of protein for early pregnancy and 1.52 g/kg/d for late pregnancy.

281
00:35:46,661 --> 00:35:58,273
These levels are higher than the current recommendation of 0.88 g/kg/d. Dietary protein intake is discussed in Chapter 10 (p. 185).

282
00:35:58,273 --> 00:36:01,373
# Carbohydrate Metabolism.

283
00:36:01,373 --> 00:36:09,886
Normal pregnancy is characterized by mild fasting hypoglycemia, postprandial hyperglycemia, and hyperinsulinemia .

284
00:36:09,886 --> 00:36:18,373
This elevated basal level of plasma insulin in normal pregnancy is associated with several unique responses to glucose ingestion.

285
00:36:18,373 --> 00:36:28,111
Specifically, after an oral glucose meal, gravidas demonstrate prolonged hyperglycemia and hyperinsulinemia and a greater suppression of glucagon.

286
00:36:28,111 --> 00:36:36,073
This cannot be explained by an increased metabolism of insulin because its half-life during pregnancy is not changed appreciably .

287
00:36:36,073 --> 00:36:46,398
Instead, this response reflects a pregnancy-induced state of peripheral insulin resistance, which ensures a sustained postprandial supply of glucose to the fetus.

288
00:36:46,398 --> 00:36:54,061
Indeed, insulin sensitivity in late normal pregnancy is 30 to 70 percent lower than that of nonpregnant women .

289
00:36:54,061 --> 00:36:56,411
Figure 4-3.

290
00:36:56,411 --> 00:37:01,523
Diurnal changes in plasma glucose and insulin in normal late pregnancy.

291
00:37:01,523 --> 00:37:09,311
The mechanisms responsible for this reduced insulin sensitivity include numerous endocrine and inflammatory factors .

292
00:37:09,311 --> 00:37:29,798
In particular, pregnancy-related hormones such as progesterone, placentally derived growth hormone, prolactin, and cortisol; cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor; and hormones derived from central adiposity, particularly leptin and its interplay with prolactin, all have a role in the insulin resistance of pregnancy.

293
00:37:29,798 --> 00:37:36,473
Even so, insulin resistance is not the only factor to elevate postprandial glucose values.

294
00:37:36,473 --> 00:37:45,073
Hepatic gluconeogenesis is augmented during both diabetic and nondiabetic pregnancies, particularly in the third trimester .

295
00:37:45,073 --> 00:37:58,923
When fasting overnight, the pregnant woman changes from a postprandial state characterized by elevated and sustained glucose levels to a fasting state characterized by lower levels of plasma glucose and some amino acids.

296
00:37:58,923 --> 00:38:07,148
Instead, plasma concentrations of free fatty acids, triglycerides, and cholesterol are higher in the fasting state.

297
00:38:07,148 --> 00:38:11,823
This pregnancy-induced switch in fuels from glucose to lipids has

298
00:38:11,980 --> 00:38:14,830
been called accelerated starvation.

299
00:38:14,780 --> 00:38:22,967
Certainly, when fasting is prolonged in the pregnant woman, these alterations are exaggerated and ketonemia rapidly appears.

300
00:38:22,967 --> 00:38:25,542
# Fat Metabolism.

301
00:38:25,542 --> 00:38:35,517
The concentrations of lipids, lipoproteins, and apolipoproteins in plasma rise appreciably during pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1231).

302
00:38:35,517 --> 00:38:43,155
Increased insulin resistance and estrogen stimulation during pregnancy are responsible for maternal hyperlipidemia.

303
00:38:43,155 --> 00:38:50,580
Augmented lipid synthesis and food intake contribute to maternal fat accumulation during the first two trimesters.

304
00:38:50,580 --> 00:38:55,580
Overweight women who have excessive gestational weight gain accrue fat mass .

305
00:38:55,580 --> 00:39:00,742
In the third trimester, however, fat storage declines or ceases.

306
00:39:00,742 --> 00:39:11,305
This is a consequence of enhanced lipolytic activity, and decreased lipoprotein lipase activity reduces circulating triglyceride uptake into adipose tissue.

307
00:39:11,305 --> 00:39:20,280
This transition to a catabolic state favors maternal use of lipids as an energy source and spares glucose and amino acids for the fetus.

308
00:39:20,280 --> 00:39:27,842
Maternal hyperlipidemia is one of the most consistent and striking changes of lipid metabolism during late pregnancy.

309
00:39:27,842 --> 00:39:44,530
Triacylglycerol and cholesterol levels in very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDLs), low-density lipoproteins (LDLs), and high-density lipoproteins (HDLs) are increased during the third trimester compared with those in nonpregnant women .

310
00:39:44,530 --> 00:39:51,380
After delivery, the concentrations of these lipids, lipoproteins, and apolipoproteins decline.

311
00:39:51,380 --> 00:39:58,467
Breastfeeding drops maternal triglyceride levels but increases those of HDL cholesterol (HDL-C).

312
00:39:58,467 --> 00:40:03,742
The effects on total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol levels are unclear .

313
00:40:03,742 --> 00:40:06,042
Table 4-3.

314
00:40:06,042 --> 00:40:14,205
Plasma Concentrations of Lipids Hyperlipidemia is theoretically a concern because it is associated with endothelial dysfunction.

315
00:40:14,205 --> 00:40:20,717
That said, endothelium-dependent vasodilation responses actually improve across pregnancy .

316
00:40:20,717 --> 00:40:29,130
This is partly because increased HDL-C concentrations likely inhibit LDL oxidation and thus protect the endothelium.

317
00:40:29,130 --> 00:40:38,492
These findings suggest that the increased cardiovascular disease risk in multiparas may be related to factors other than maternal hypercholesterolemia.

318
00:40:38,492 --> 00:40:40,567
# Leptin.

319
00:40:40,567 --> 00:40:46,630
This peptide hormone is primarily secreted by adipose tissue in nonpregnant humans.

320
00:40:46,630 --> 00:40:52,692
It plays a key role in body fat and energy expenditure regulation and in reproduction.

321
00:40:52,692 --> 00:40:59,542
For example, leptin is important for implantation, cell proliferation, and angiogenesis .

322
00:40:59,542 --> 00:41:07,630
Leptin deficiency is associated with anovulation and infertility, whereas certain leptin mutations cause extreme obesity .

323
00:41:07,630 --> 00:41:18,867
Among normal-weight gravidas, serum leptin levels rise and peak during the second trimester and plateau until term in concentrations two to four times higher than those in nonpregnant women.

324
00:41:18,867 --> 00:41:23,255
Among obese women, leptin levels correlate with adiposity .

325
00:41:23,255 --> 00:41:30,680
In all cases, leptin levels fall after delivery, reflecting the significant amounts produced by the placenta .

326
00:41:30,680 --> 00:41:35,855
Leptin participates in regulating energy metabolism during pregnancy.

327
00:41:35,855 --> 00:41:44,630
Despite the rise in leptin concentrations during pregnancy, reduced leptin sensitivity to food intake during pregnancy has been described .

328
00:41:44,630 --> 00:41:51,317
This “leptin resistance” may serve to promote energy storage during pregnancy and for later lactation.

329
00:41:51,317 --> 00:41:58,742
Higher leptin levels during pregnancy may be disadvantageous under certain situations, such as maternal obesity.

330
00:41:58,742 --> 00:42:09,580
Leptin functions as a proinflammatory cytokine in white adipose tissue, which may dysregulate the inflammatory cascade and lead to placental dysfunction in obese women .

331
00:42:09,580 --> 00:42:22,392
In addition, abnormally elevated leptin levels have been associated with preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, and fetal distress (Rabiepoor, 2019; Taylor, 2015).

332
00:42:22,392 --> 00:42:29,842
Fetal leptin is important for the development of several organs that include the pancreas, kidney, heart, and brain.

333
00:42:29,842 --> 00:42:36,267
Fetal levels correlate with maternal body mass index and birthweight (Özdemir, 2020).

334
00:42:36,267 --> 00:42:39,830
Lower levels are linked to fetal-growth restriction .

335
00:42:39,830 --> 00:42:42,855
# Other Adipocytokines.

336
00:42:42,855 --> 00:42:49,230
Dozens of hormones with metabolic and/or inflammatory functions are produced by adipose tissue.

337
00:42:49,230 --> 00:42:54,955
Adiponectin is a peptide produced primarily in maternal fat but not in the placenta .

338
00:42:54,955 --> 00:42:56,917
It may play a

339
00:42:57,076 --> 00:43:02,138
role in early pregnancy to assure a source of glycogen for fetal energy .

340
00:43:02,088 --> 00:43:09,101
Adiponectin levels inversely correlate with adiposity, and it acts as a potent insulin sensitizer.

341
00:43:09,101 --> 00:43:18,226
Despite reduced adiponectin levels in women with gestational diabetes, directed assays are not useful for predicting diabetes development .

342
00:43:18,226 --> 00:43:23,076
Ghrelin is a peptide secreted mainly by the stomach in response to hunger.

343
00:43:23,076 --> 00:43:29,551
It cooperates with other neuroendocrine factors, such as leptin, in energy homeostasis.

344
00:43:29,551 --> 00:43:38,926
Ghrelin is also expressed in the placenta and likely has a role in fetal growth and cell proliferation (González-Domínguez, 2016).

345
00:43:38,926 --> 00:43:47,076
Visfatin is a peptide that was first identified as a growth factor for B lymphocytes but is mainly produced within adipose tissue.

346
00:43:47,076 --> 00:43:55,688
Mumtaz and associates (2015) propose that elevated levels of visfatin and leptin impair uterine contractility.

347
00:43:55,688 --> 00:44:03,926
Such findings may provide a physiological basis for the observation that maternal obesity raises the risk for dysfunctional labor.

348
00:44:03,926 --> 00:44:07,388
# Electrolyte and Mineral Metabolism.

349
00:44:07,388 --> 00:44:15,726
During normal pregnancy, nearly 1000 mEq of sodium and 300 mEq of potassium are retained .

350
00:44:15,726 --> 00:44:26,713
The glomerular filtration rate of sodium and potassium is increased, but the excretion of these electrolytes is unchanged during pregnancy as a result of enhanced tubular resorption .

351
00:44:26,713 --> 00:44:36,788
Although total accumulations of sodium and potassium are elevated, their serum concentrations are diminished slightly (Appendix, p. 1229).

352
00:44:36,788 --> 00:44:40,576
Several mechanisms may explain these lower levels .

353
00:44:40,576 --> 00:44:46,701
In the case of potassium, it possibly involves the expanded plasma volume of pregnancy.

354
00:44:46,701 --> 00:44:54,151
With respect to sodium, osmoregulation is altered, and the threshold for arginine vasopressin release is lowered.

355
00:44:54,151 --> 00:44:58,551
This promotes free water retention and diminished sodium levels.

356
00:44:58,551 --> 00:45:06,088
Total serum calcium levels, which include both ionized and nonionized calcium, decrease during pregnancy.

357
00:45:06,088 --> 00:45:15,901
This reduction follows lowered plasma albumin concentrations and in turn a consequent decline in the amount of circulating protein-bound nonionized calcium.

358
00:45:15,901 --> 00:45:20,701
Serum ionized calcium levels, however, remain unchanged .

359
00:45:20,701 --> 00:45:26,726
The developing fetus imposes a significant demand on maternal calcium homeostasis.

360
00:45:26,726 --> 00:45:35,938
For example, the fetal skeleton accrues approximately 30 g of calcium by term, 80 percent of which is deposited during the third trimester.

361
00:45:35,938 --> 00:45:48,013
This demand is largely met by a doubling of maternal intestinal calcium absorption mediated partly by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3, the serum levels of which also are doubled.

362
00:45:48,013 --> 00:46:00,238
These higher levels of vitamin D are possibly stimulated by a twofold rise in parathyroid hormone (PTH)-related peptide levels produced by several tissues including the placenta .

363
00:46:00,238 --> 00:46:07,751
To help compensate, dietary intake of sufficient calcium is necessary to prevent excess depletion from the mother.

364
00:46:07,751 --> 00:46:14,576
A list of all recommended daily allowances is found in Table 10-5 (p. 184).

365
00:46:14,576 --> 00:46:20,488
This is especially important for pregnant adolescents, in whom bones are still developing .

366
00:46:20,488 --> 00:46:33,513
Unfortunately, a lack of robust data prevents drawing firm conclusions regarding the utility of calcium and vitamin D supplements during pregnancy (Bi, 2018; Hofmeyr, 2018).

367
00:46:33,513 --> 00:46:37,751
Serum magnesium levels also decline during pregnancy.

368
00:46:37,751 --> 00:46:46,388
Compared with nonpregnant women, both total and ionized magnesium concentrations are significantly lower during normal pregnancy .

369
00:46:46,388 --> 00:46:50,526
Serum phosphate levels lie within the nonpregnant range .

370
00:46:50,526 --> 00:47:00,051
Although calcitonin is an important regulator of serum calcium and phosphate, the importance of calcitonin as it relates to pregnancy is poorly understood .

371
00:47:00,051 --> 00:47:05,101
Iodine requirements increase during normal pregnancy for several reasons .

372
00:47:05,101 --> 00:47:16,413
First, maternal thyroxine (T4) production rises to maintain maternal euthyroidism and to transfer thyroid hormone to the fetus prior to fetal thyroid functioning.

373
00:47:16,413 --> 00:47:22,288
Second, fetal thyroid hormone production increases during the second half of pregnancy.

374
00:47:22,288 --> 00:47:28,863
This contributes to greater maternal iodine requirements because iodine readily crosses the placenta.

375
00:47:28,863 --> 00:47:33,226
Third, the primary route of iodine excretion is the kidney.

376
00:47:33,226 --> 00:47:40,363
Beginning in early pregnancy, the iodine glomerular filtration rate increases by 30 to 50 percent.

377
00:47:40,516 --> 00:47:52,016
In sum, because of greater thyroid hormone production, fetal iodine requirements, and augmented renal clearance, dietary iodine needs are higher during normal gestation .

378
00:47:51,966 --> 00:48:02,316
Although the placenta has the ability to store iodine, whether this organ functions to protect the fetus from inadequate maternal dietary iodine is currently unknown.

379
00:48:02,316 --> 00:48:10,891
Iodine deficiency is discussed later in this chapter (p. 72) and in Chapter 61 (p. 1097).

380
00:48:10,891 --> 00:48:16,753
Extremely low or high maternal iodine intake may affect childhood neurodevelopment .

381
00:48:16,753 --> 00:48:24,366
At the extreme, maternal supplements containing excessive iodine have been associated with congenital hypothyroidism.

382
00:48:24,366 --> 00:48:34,366
This stems from autoregulation in the thyroid gland—known as the Wolff-Chaikoff effect—to curb T4 production in response to iodine overconsumption.

383
00:48:34,366 --> 00:48:40,466
The serum levels of many of these minerals are listed in the Appendix (p. 1229).

384
00:48:40,466 --> 00:48:49,953
With respect to most other minerals, pregnancy induces little change in their metabolism other than their retention in amounts equivalent to those needed for growth.

385
00:48:49,953 --> 00:48:56,528
An important exception is the considerably greater requirement for iron, which is discussed subsequently .

386
00:48:56,528 --> 00:48:58,978
Hematological Changes.

387
00:48:58,978 --> 00:49:01,316
# Blood Volume.

388
00:49:01,316 --> 00:49:16,241
The well-known hypervolemia associated with normal pregnancy averages 40 to 45 percent above the nonpregnant blood volume after 32 to 34 weeks’ gestation (Pritchard, 1965; Zeeman, 2009).

389
00:49:16,241 --> 00:49:23,128
In some, accumulated volume rises only modestly, whereas in others blood volume nearly doubles.

390
00:49:23,128 --> 00:49:29,378
A fetus is not essential, as augmented blood volume develops in some with hydatidiform mole.

391
00:49:29,378 --> 00:49:37,091
The stimulus is not known, but it likely is related to renin and prorenin with its effects on sodium and water retention .

392
00:49:37,091 --> 00:49:42,591
Simultaneously, vascular plasticity accommodates the larger blood volume .

393
00:49:42,591 --> 00:49:46,666
Pregnancy-induced hypervolemia serves several functions.

394
00:49:46,666 --> 00:49:53,566
First, it meets the metabolic demands of the enlarged uterus and its greatly hypertrophied vascular system.

395
00:49:53,566 --> 00:49:59,916
It also provides abundant nutrients and elements to support the rapidly growing placenta and fetus.

396
00:49:59,916 --> 00:50:10,678
The expanded intravascular volume protects the mother, and in turn the fetus, against the deleterious effects of impaired venous return in the supine and erect positions.

397
00:50:10,678 --> 00:50:17,016
Last, it safeguards the mother against the adverse effects of parturition-associated blood loss.

398
00:50:17,016 --> 00:50:21,491
Maternal blood volume begins to accrue during the first trimester.

399
00:50:21,491 --> 00:50:29,491
By 12 menstrual weeks, plasma volume expands by approximately 15 percent compared with that prior to pregnancy .

400
00:50:29,491 --> 00:50:40,928
Maternal blood volume grows most rapidly during the midtrimester, rises at a much slower rate during the third trimester, and reaches a plateau during the last several weeks of pregnancy .

401
00:50:40,928 --> 00:50:45,428
Blood volume accrues even more dramatically in twin gestations.

402
00:50:45,428 --> 00:50:47,841
Figure 4-4.

403
00:50:47,841 --> 00:50:55,228
Blood volume expansion during pregnancy in twins (n = 10) and singletons (n = 40).

404
00:50:55,228 --> 00:50:57,566
Data shown as medians.

405
00:50:57,566 --> 00:51:03,216
During blood volume expansion, plasma volume and erythrocyte mass increase.

406
00:51:03,216 --> 00:51:14,028
Although more plasma than erythrocytes is usually added to the maternal circulation, the increase in erythrocyte volume is considerable and averages 450 mL .

407
00:51:14,028 --> 00:51:22,378
Moderate erythroid hyperplasia develops in the bone marrow, and the reticulocyte count is elevated slightly during normal pregnancy.

408
00:51:22,378 --> 00:51:31,253
These changes are almost certainly related to the rise in maternal plasma erythropoietin levels (Appendix, p. 1227).

409
00:51:31,253 --> 00:51:39,016
Because of great plasma augmentation, both hemoglobin concentration and hematocrit decline slightly during pregnancy.

410
00:51:39,016 --> 00:51:42,866
As a result, whole blood viscosity decreases .

411
00:51:42,866 --> 00:51:53,878
Hemoglobin concentration at term averages 12.5 g/dL, and in approximately 5 percent of women it is below 11.0 g/dL.

412
00:51:53,878 --> 00:52:07,028
Thus, a concentration <11.0 g/dL, especially late in pregnancy, is considered abnormal and usually due to iron-deficiency anemia rather than pregnancy hypervolemia .

413
00:52:07,028 --> 00:52:09,603
# Iron Metabolism.

414
00:52:09,603 --> 00:52:18,966
The total iron content of normal adult women ranges from 2.0 to 2.5 g, or approximately half that found normally in men.

415
00:52:18,966 --> 00:52:28,103
Most of this is incorporated in hemoglobin or myoglobin, and thus iron stores of normal young women approximate only 300 mg .

416
00:52:28,103 --> 00:52:32,053
Although the lower iron levels in women may be partly due

417
00:52:32,212 --> 00:52:35,949
to menstrual blood loss, other factors have a role.

418
00:52:35,899 --> 00:52:43,374
One is hepcidin—a peptide hormone that functions as a homeostatic regulator of systemic iron metabolism .

419
00:52:43,374 --> 00:52:51,037
As shown in Figure 45, hepcidin levels drop early in pregnancy (Hedengran, 2016a,b).

420
00:52:51,037 --> 00:52:57,699
Lower hepcidin levels are associated with greater absorption of iron via ferroportin in enterocytes .

421
00:52:57,699 --> 00:53:05,049
Lower hepcidin levels also augment iron transport into the fetus via ferroportin in syncytiotrophoblast.

422
00:53:05,049 --> 00:53:17,324
Hepcidin levels rise with inflammation, but drop with iron deficiency and increased amount of several hormones, including testosterone, estrogen, vitamin D, and possibly prolactin .

423
00:53:17,324 --> 00:53:19,762
Figure 4-5.

424
00:53:19,762 --> 00:53:22,587
Hepcidin levels during pregnancy.

425
00:53:22,587 --> 00:53:28,974
Blue data points are median values and crossbars are the 25th and 75th percentiles.

426
00:53:28,974 --> 00:53:34,449
D is delivery and D + 1 and D + 2 are postpartum days 1 and 2.

427
00:53:34,449 --> 00:53:51,499
Of the approximate 1000 mg of iron required for normal pregnancy, about 300 mg is actively transferred to the fetus and placenta, and another 200 mg is lost through various normal excretion routes, primarily the gastrointestinal tract.

428
00:53:51,499 --> 00:53:56,487
These are obligatory losses and accrue even when the mother is iron deficient.

429
00:53:56,487 --> 00:54:11,012
The average increase in the total circulating erythrocyte volume— about 450 mL—requires another 500 mg. Recall that each 1 mL of erythrocytes contains 1.1 mg of iron.

430
00:54:11,012 --> 00:54:17,212
As shown in Figure 4-6, most iron is used during the latter half of pregnancy .

431
00:54:17,212 --> 00:54:24,462
Thus, the iron requirement becomes large after midpregnancy and averages 6 to 7 mg/d .

432
00:54:24,462 --> 00:54:29,987
In most women, this amount is usually not available from iron stores or diet.

433
00:54:29,987 --> 00:54:38,887
If the nonanemic pregnant woman is not given supplemental iron, serum iron and ferritin concentrations decline after midpregnancy.

434
00:54:38,887 --> 00:54:49,912
Moreover, the optimal rise in maternal erythrocyte volume will not develop, and the hemoglobin concentration and hematocrit will fall appreciably as plasma volume rises.

435
00:54:49,912 --> 00:54:59,037
At the same time, fetal red cell production is not impaired because the placenta transfers iron even if the mother has marked iron-deficiency anemia.

436
00:54:59,037 --> 00:55:11,399
In severe cases, we have documented maternal hemoglobin values of 3 g/dL, and at the same time, fetuses had hemoglobin concentrations of 16 g/dL.

437
00:55:11,399 --> 00:55:20,399
The mechanisms of placental iron transport and regulation are complex (Koenig, 2014; McArdle, 2014).

438
00:55:20,399 --> 00:55:22,887
Figure 4-6.

439
00:55:22,887 --> 00:55:28,762
Estimated daily iron requirements during pregnancy in a 55-kg woman.

440
00:55:28,762 --> 00:55:39,687
With normal vaginal delivery, at least 500 to 600 mL of blood is typically lost, and thus not all the maternal iron added in the form of hemoglobin is spent.

441
00:55:39,687 --> 00:55:44,074
The excess hemoglobin iron postpartum becomes stored iron.

442
00:55:44,074 --> 00:55:47,024
# Immunological Functions.

443
00:55:47,024 --> 00:55:53,687
Pregnancy is associated with suppression of various humoral and cellmediated immunological functions .

444
00:55:53,687 --> 00:56:01,974
This permits accommodation of the “foreign” semiallogeneic fetal graft that contains antigens of both maternal and paternal origin .

445
00:56:01,974 --> 00:56:08,137
The tolerance that exists at the maternal-fetal interface remains a great unsolved medical mystery.

446
00:56:08,137 --> 00:56:18,212
This tolerance is complex and involves certain immune system adaptations and crosstalk among the maternal microbiome, uterine decidua, and trophoblast.

447
00:56:18,212 --> 00:56:24,974
In particular, areas of the uterus that were previously considered sterile are colonized with bacteria.

448
00:56:24,974 --> 00:56:31,549
In most cases, these microbes are believed to be commensal and play a tolerizing and protective role.

449
00:56:31,549 --> 00:56:37,149
Indeed, commensal organisms may inhibit the proliferation of certain pathogens.

450
00:56:37,149 --> 00:56:46,999
Several reviewers have described these relationships (Mor, 2015; Racicot, 2014; Sisti, 2016).

451
00:56:46,999 --> 00:56:59,799
One immune adaptation that promotes tolerance and protection at the maternal-fetal interface involves the expression of special major histocompatibility complex (MHC) molecules on the trophoblast.

452
00:56:59,799 --> 00:57:08,674
Another immune adaptation that promotes tolerance stems from important changes in CD4 T lymphocyte subpopulations in pregnancy.

453
00:57:08,674 --> 00:57:16,512
Specifically, immunity mediated by T-helper (Th) 1 cells shifts to Th2-mediated immunity.

454
00:57:16,512 --> 00:57:32,824
An important antiinflammatory component of pregnancy involves suppression of Th1 and T-cytotoxic (Tc) 1 cells, which lower secretion of interleukin 2 (IL2), interferon α, and tumor necrosis factor (TNF).

455
00:57:32,824 --> 00:57:34,999
In cervical mucus,

456
00:57:35,164 --> 00:57:52,439
peak levels of immunoglobulins A and G (IgA and IgG) are significantly higher during pregnancy, and the immunoglobulin-rich cervical mucus plug creates a barrier to ascending infection (Hansen, 2014; Wang, 2014).

457
00:57:52,439 --> 00:58:02,426
Similarly, IgG is transferred to the developing fetus in the third trimester as a form of passive immunity, ostensibly in anticipation of birth.

458
00:58:02,426 --> 00:58:09,326
Immunoglobulins secreted into breast milk during lactation augment neonatal defenses against infection .

459
00:58:09,326 --> 00:58:12,501
# Leukocytes and Lymphocytes.

460
00:58:12,501 --> 00:58:24,276
Normal leukocyte counts during pregnancy can be higher than nonpregnant values, and upper values approach 15,000/μL (Appendix, p. 1227).

461
00:58:24,276 --> 00:58:33,864
During labor and the early puerperium, values may markedly rise to attain levels ≥25,000/μL .

462
00:58:33,864 --> 00:58:39,926
The cause is unknown, but the same response occurs during and after strenuous exercise.

463
00:58:39,926 --> 00:58:47,276
The leukocytosis possibly represents the reappearance of leukocytes previously shunted out of active circulation.

464
00:58:47,276 --> 00:58:52,326
The distribution of lymphocyte cell types also is altered during pregnancy.

465
00:58:52,326 --> 00:59:00,964
Specifically, B lymphocytes numbers are unchanged, but the absolute numbers of T lymphocytes rise and create a relative increase.

466
00:59:00,964 --> 00:59:08,726
Concurrently, the ratio of CD4 to CD8 T lymphocytes does not change (Kühnert, 1998).

467
00:59:08,726 --> 00:59:11,614
# Inflammatory Markers.

468
00:59:11,614 --> 00:59:17,751
Many tests performed to diagnose inflammation cannot be used reliably during pregnancy.

469
00:59:17,751 --> 00:59:27,576
For example, leukocyte alkaline phosphatase levels— used to evaluate myeloproliferative disorders—are elevated beginning early in pregnancy.

470
00:59:27,576 --> 00:59:36,714
The concentration of C-reactive protein, an acute-phase serum reactant, rises rapidly in response to tissue trauma or inflammation.

471
00:59:36,714 --> 00:59:42,926
Median C-reactive protein levels in pregnancy and labor are higher than for nonpregnant women .

472
00:59:42,926 --> 00:59:54,339
Of nonlaboring gravidas, 95 percent had levels measuring ≤1.5 mg/dL, and gestational age did not affect serum levels.

473
00:59:54,339 --> 01:00:05,876
Another marker of inflammation, the erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), is increased in normal pregnancy because of elevated plasma globulin and fibrinogen concentrations.

474
01:00:05,876 --> 01:00:13,114
Complement factors C3 and C4 levels also significantly rise during the second and third trimesters .

475
01:00:13,114 --> 01:00:25,076
Concentrations of procalcitonin, a normal precursor of calcitonin, are low to undetectable in midpregnancy but increase at the end of the third trimester and through the first few postpartum days .

476
01:00:25,076 --> 01:00:33,814
Procalcitonin levels rise with severe bacterial infections but remain low in viral infections and nonspecific inflammatory disease.

477
01:00:33,814 --> 01:00:46,564
However, measured levels poorly predict development of overt or subclinical chorioamnionitis after premature rupture of membranes (Thornburg, 2016; Tujula, 2018).

478
01:00:46,564 --> 01:00:50,251
# Coagulation and Fibrinolysis.

479
01:00:50,251 --> 01:00:58,464
During normal pregnancy, both coagulation and fibrinolysis are augmented but remain balanced to maintain hemostasis .

480
01:00:58,464 --> 01:01:05,951
Evidence of activation includes increased concentrations of all clotting factors except factors XI and XIII .

481
01:01:05,951 --> 01:01:08,301
Table 4-4.

482
01:01:08,301 --> 01:01:18,464
Coagulation Factor Normal Values Across Pregnancy Of procoagulants, the level and rate of thrombin generation throughout gestation progressively increase .

483
01:01:18,464 --> 01:01:30,014
In normal nonpregnant women, plasma fibrinogen (factor I) averages 300 mg/dL and ranges from 200 to 400 mg/dL.

484
01:01:30,014 --> 01:01:36,251
During normal pregnancy, the fibrinogen concentration rises approximately 50 percent.

485
01:01:36,251 --> 01:01:45,439
In late pregnancy, it averages 450 mg/dL, with a range from 300 to 600 mg/dL.

486
01:01:45,439 --> 01:01:52,151
As discussed, this contributes greatly to the striking elevation in the erythrocyte sedimentation rate.

487
01:01:52,151 --> 01:02:00,314
Also, levels of factor XIII—fibrin stabilizing factor—significantly drop as normal pregnancy advances .

488
01:02:00,314 --> 01:02:05,364
Few studies describe thromboelastographic changes in normal pregnancy .

489
01:02:05,364 --> 01:02:14,364
The end product of the coagulation cascade is fibrin formation, and the main function of the fibrinolytic system is to remove excess fibrin.

490
01:02:14,364 --> 01:02:23,114
Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) converts plasminogen into plasmin, which is the enzyme that promotes fibrinolysis.

491
01:02:23,114 --> 01:02:29,789
It yields fibrin-degradation products such as D-dimers, and levels of these are increased in pregnancy .

492
01:02:29,789 --> 01:02:37,101
Although somewhat conflicting, most evidence suggests that fibrinolytic activity is reduced in normal pregnancy .

493
01:02:37,101 --> 01:02:39,651
As reviewed by Cunningham and

494
01:02:39,820 --> 01:02:45,070
Nelson (2015), these changes favor fibrin formation.

495
01:02:45,020 --> 01:02:52,395
Although this is countered by increased levels of plasminogen, the net result is that pregnancy is a procoagulant state.

496
01:02:52,395 --> 01:02:56,670
Amnionic fluid is a potent activation of coagulation .

497
01:02:56,670 --> 01:03:05,720
Such changes serve to ensure hemostatic control during pregnancy, particularly during delivery when a certain amount of blood loss is expected.

498
01:03:05,720 --> 01:03:08,707
# Regulatory Proteins.

499
01:03:08,707 --> 01:03:16,257
Several proteins are natural inhibitors of coagulation, including proteins C and S and antithrombin.

500
01:03:16,257 --> 01:03:28,332
Inherited or acquired deficiencies of these and other natural regulatory proteins—collectively referred to as thrombophilias— account for many thromboembolic episodes during pregnancy.

501
01:03:28,332 --> 01:03:33,170
They are discussed in Chapter 55 (p. 976).

502
01:03:33,170 --> 01:03:45,382
Activated protein C, along with the cofactors protein S and factor V, functions as an anticoagulant by neutralizing the procoagulants factor Va and factor VIIIa.

503
01:03:45,382 --> 01:03:56,520
During pregnancy, resistance to activated protein C grows progressively and is related to a concomitant drop in free protein S levels and greater factor VIII concentrations.

504
01:03:56,520 --> 01:04:15,145
Between the first and third trimesters, activated protein C levels decline from 2.4 to 1.9 U/mL, and free protein S concentrations diminish from 0.4 to 0.16 U/mL (Cunningham, 2015; Walker, 1997).

505
01:04:15,145 --> 01:04:24,457
Antithrombin levels drop by 13 percent between midpregnancy and term and fall 30 percent from this baseline until 12 hours after delivery.

506
01:04:24,457 --> 01:04:29,320
By 72 hours after delivery, there is a return to baseline .

507
01:04:29,320 --> 01:04:31,532
# Platelets.

508
01:04:31,532 --> 01:04:44,557
In a study of more than 7000 women, and as shown in Figure 4-7, the average platelet count declined across pregnancy and returned to normal nonpregnant values by 4 to 12 weeks postpartum .

509
01:04:44,557 --> 01:04:48,195
The platelet counts were lower for twin pregnancies.

510
01:04:48,195 --> 01:04:52,657
Lower platelet concentrations are partially due to hemodilution.

511
01:04:52,657 --> 01:05:00,570
In addition, platelet consumption is likely augmented and creates a greater proportion of younger and therefore larger platelets .

512
01:05:00,570 --> 01:05:08,957
Further, clinically insignificant levels of several markers of platelet activation rise with gestational age but drop postpartum .

513
01:05:08,957 --> 01:05:16,320
Because of splenic enlargement, an element of “hypersplenism,” may sequester and prematurely destroy platelets .

514
01:05:16,320 --> 01:05:18,745
Figure 4-7.

515
01:05:18,745 --> 01:05:28,895
Platelet counts in singleton and twin pregnancies determined during 1st, 2nd, and 3rd trimester, at delivery, and then at 4–12 weeks postpartum.

516
01:05:28,895 --> 01:05:31,532
NP = nonpregnant.

517
01:05:31,532 --> 01:05:35,932
Values are means and 95% confidence intervals.

518
01:05:35,932 --> 01:05:37,982
# Spleen.

519
01:05:37,982 --> 01:05:45,170
By the end of normal pregnancy, the spleen enlarges up to 50 percent compared with that in the first trimester .

520
01:05:45,170 --> 01:05:54,632
Moreover, Gayer and coworkers (2012) found that splenic size was 68-percent greater compared with that of nonpregnant controls.

521
01:05:54,632 --> 01:06:03,007
The cause of this splenomegaly is unknown, but it might follow the increased blood volume and/or the hemodynamic changes of pregnancy.

522
01:06:03,007 --> 01:06:05,420
Cardiovascular System.

523
01:06:05,420 --> 01:06:10,945
Changes in cardiac function become apparent during the first 8 weeks of pregnancy .

524
01:06:10,945 --> 01:06:19,320
Cardiac output is increased as early as the fifth week and reflects a reduced systemic vascular resistance and an increased heart rate.

525
01:06:19,320 --> 01:06:32,357
Compared with prepregnancy measurements, brachial systolic blood pressure, diastolic blood pressure, and central systolic blood pressure are all significantly lower 6 to 7 weeks from the last menstrual period .

526
01:06:32,357 --> 01:06:37,820
The resting pulse rate rises approximately 10 beats/min during pregnancy.

527
01:06:37,820 --> 01:06:50,482
Nelson and associates (2015) found that for both normal and overweight women, heart rate rose significantly between 12 and 16 weeks’ and between 32 and 36 weeks’ gestation.

528
01:06:50,482 --> 01:06:56,557
Between weeks 10 and 20, plasma volume expansion begins, and preload rises.

529
01:06:56,557 --> 01:07:03,145
This augmented preload results in a significantly larger left atrial volume and ejection fraction .

530
01:07:03,145 --> 01:07:12,395
Ventricular performance during pregnancy is influenced by both the decline in systemic vascular resistance and changes in pulsatile arterial flow.

531
01:07:12,395 --> 01:07:23,845
Multiple factors contribute to this overall altered hemodynamic function, which allows the physiological demands of the fetus to be met while maintaining maternal cardiovascular integrity .

532
01:07:23,845 --> 01:07:30,395
These changes in stroke volume during the last half of pregnancy and the effects of maternal posture are

533
01:07:30,556 --> 01:07:33,581
summarized in Figure 4-8 .

534
01:07:33,531 --> 01:07:41,256
Bijl and colleagues (2019) offer a detailed review of methods to measure cardiac function in gravidas.

535
01:07:41,256 --> 01:07:43,543
Figure 4-8.

536
01:07:43,543 --> 01:07:54,106
Left ventricular stroke volume across pregnancy compared with 12-week postpartum (nonpregnant) values for normal-weight women in the supine and lateral positions.

537
01:07:54,106 --> 01:07:56,043
# Heart.

538
01:07:56,043 --> 01:08:04,168
As the diaphragm becomes progressively elevated, the heart is displaced to the left and upward and is rotated on its long axis.

539
01:08:04,168 --> 01:08:13,156
As a result, the apex is moved somewhat laterally from its usual position and produces a larger cardiac silhouette in chest radiographs.

540
01:08:13,156 --> 01:08:21,068
Furthermore, gravidas normally have some degree of benign pericardial effusion, which may enlarge the cardiac silhouette .

541
01:08:21,068 --> 01:08:28,918
These factors make it difficult to precisely identify moderate degrees of cardiomegaly by simple radiographic studies.

542
01:08:28,918 --> 01:08:40,193
Normal pregnancy induces some characteristic electrocardiographic (ECG) changes, and the most common is slight left-axis deviation due to the altered heart position.

543
01:08:40,193 --> 01:08:49,206
Q waves in leads II, III, and avF and flat or inverted T waves in leads III and V1–V3 also may be seen .

544
01:08:49,206 --> 01:08:53,981
During pregnancy, many of the normal cardiac sounds are modified.

545
01:08:53,981 --> 01:09:09,431
These include: (1) an exaggerated splitting of the first heart sound and increased loudness of both components, (2) no definite changes in the aortic and pulmonary elements of the second sound, and (3) a loud, easily heard third sound .

546
01:09:09,431 --> 01:09:16,781
In most gravidas, a systolic murmur is intensified during inspiration in some or with expiration in others.

547
01:09:16,781 --> 01:09:28,931
Less often, a soft diastolic murmur can be noted transiently, and continuous murmurs arising from the breast vasculature may be heard (Fig. 52-1, p. 917).

548
01:09:28,931 --> 01:09:38,706
Structurally, the expanding plasma volume seen during normal pregnancy is reflected by enlarging cardiac end-systolic and end-diastolic dimensions.

549
01:09:38,706 --> 01:09:44,168
Concurrently, however, septal thickness or ejection fraction does not change.

550
01:09:44,168 --> 01:09:55,843
This is because these dimensional changes are accompanied by substantive ventricular remodeling, which is characterized by left-ventricular mass expansion of 30 to 35 percent near term.

551
01:09:55,843 --> 01:10:03,631
In the nonpregnant state, the heart is capable of remodeling in response to stimuli such as hypertension and exercise.

552
01:10:03,631 --> 01:10:17,231
Such cardiac plasticity likely is a continuum that encompasses physiological growth as with exercise, and pathological hypertrophy as with hypertension (Hill, 2008; Osol, 2019).

553
01:10:17,231 --> 01:10:26,281
Stewart and colleagues (2016) used cardiac MR imaging to prospectively evaluate cardiac remodeling during pregnancy.

554
01:10:26,281 --> 01:10:36,156
Compared with the first trimester, left ventricular mass significantly grew beginning at 26 to 30 weeks’ gestation, and this continued until delivery .

555
01:10:36,156 --> 01:10:44,818
This remodeling is concentric and proportional to maternal size for both normal and overweight women and resolved within 3 months of delivery.

556
01:10:44,818 --> 01:10:53,593
Certainly for clinical purposes, ventricular function during pregnancy is normal, as estimated by the Braunwald ventricular function graph .

557
01:10:53,593 --> 01:11:01,393
For the given filling pressures, cardiac output is appropriate and thus cardiac function during pregnancy is eudynamic.

558
01:11:01,393 --> 01:11:15,281
Of the metabolic changes that occur in the heart during pregnancy, the efficiency of cardiac work—which is the product of cardiac output × mean arterial pressure—is estimated to rise by approximately 25 percent.

559
01:11:15,281 --> 01:11:24,231
The associated increase in oxygen consumption is primarily accomplished via increased coronary blood flow rather than increased extraction .

560
01:11:24,231 --> 01:11:26,618
Figure 4-9.

561
01:11:26,618 --> 01:11:35,218
Left ventricular mass of normal-weight and overweight women across pregnancy compared with 12-week postpartum (nonpregnant) values.

562
01:11:35,218 --> 01:11:37,493
Figure 4-10.

563
01:11:37,493 --> 01:11:51,031
Relationship between left ventricular stroke work index (LVSWI), cardiac output, and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) in 10 normal pregnant women in the third trimester.

564
01:11:51,031 --> 01:11:53,681
# Cardiac Output.

565
01:11:53,681 --> 01:12:01,693
When measured in the lateral recumbent position at rest, cardiac output increases significantly beginning in early pregnancy.

566
01:12:01,693 --> 01:12:06,143
It continues to rise and remains elevated during the remainder.

567
01:12:06,143 --> 01:12:13,731
In a supine woman, a large uterus consistently compresses veins and diminishes venous return from the lower body.

568
01:12:13,731 --> 01:12:16,456
It may also compress the aorta.

569
01:12:16,456 --> 01:12:17,793
In

570
01:12:17,956 --> 01:12:23,118
response, cardiac filling is limited, and cardiac output lessened.

571
01:12:23,068 --> 01:12:40,256
Specifically, cardiac MR imaging shows that a woman rolling from her back onto her left side raises her cardiac output at 26 to 30 weeks’ gestation by approximately 20 percent and at 32 to 34 weeks by 10 percent (see Fig. 4-7).

572
01:12:40,256 --> 01:12:53,181
Consistent with this, Simpson and James (2005) found that fetal oxygen saturation is approximately 10 percent higher if a laboring woman lies in a lateral recumbent position compared with supine.

573
01:12:53,181 --> 01:12:59,131
Upon standing, cardiac output falls to the same degree as in the nonpregnant woman .

574
01:12:59,131 --> 01:13:07,968
In multifetal pregnancies, compared with singletons, maternal cardiac output is augmented further by almost another 20 percent.

575
01:13:07,968 --> 01:13:23,368
Ghi and coworkers (2015) used transthoracic echocardiography to show that first-trimester cardiac output with twins (mean 5.5 L/min) was more than 20 percent greater than postpartum values.

576
01:13:23,368 --> 01:13:35,956
Cardiac output values in the second (6.3 L/min) and third (6.3 L/min) trimesters rose an additional 15 percent compared with first-trimester output.

577
01:13:35,956 --> 01:13:43,581
Due to augmented preload, left atrial and left ventricular end-diastolic diameters also grow with twins .

578
01:13:43,581 --> 01:13:51,706
The augmented heart rate and inotropic contractility imply that cardiovascular reserve is reduced in multifetal gestations .

579
01:13:51,706 --> 01:13:56,443
During first-stage labor, cardiac output rises moderately.

580
01:13:56,443 --> 01:14:02,068
During the second stage, with vigorous expulsive efforts, it is appreciably greater.

581
01:14:02,068 --> 01:14:08,243
The pregnancy-induced increase is lost after delivery, at times dependent on blood loss.

582
01:14:08,243 --> 01:14:12,131
# Hemodynamic Function in Late Pregnancy.

583
01:14:12,131 --> 01:14:20,068
Clark and associates (1989) conducted invasive studies to measure hemodynamic function late in pregnancy .

584
01:14:20,068 --> 01:14:30,093
Right heart catheterization was performed in 10 healthy nulliparas at 35 to 38 weeks’ gestation, and again at 11 to 13 weeks postpartum.

585
01:14:30,093 --> 01:14:37,393
Late pregnancy was associated with the expected increases in heart rate, stroke volume, and cardiac output.

586
01:14:37,393 --> 01:14:45,231
Systemic vascular and pulmonary vascular resistance both dropped significantly, as did colloid osmotic pressure.

587
01:14:45,231 --> 01:14:51,118
Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and central venous pressure did not change appreciably.

588
01:14:51,118 --> 01:15:02,156
Thus, although cardiac output rises, left ventricular function as measured by stroke work index remains similar to the nonpregnant normal range (see Fig. 4-10).

589
01:15:02,156 --> 01:15:07,193
Put another way, normal pregnancy is not a continuous “high-output” state .

590
01:15:07,193 --> 01:15:09,606
Table 4-5.

591
01:15:09,606 --> 01:15:17,593
Central Hemodynamic Changes in 10 Normal Nulliparous Women Near Term and Postpartum # Circulation and Blood Pressure.

592
01:15:17,593 --> 01:15:25,293
Arterial pressure usually declines to a nadir at 24 to 26 weeks’ gestation and rises thereafter.

593
01:15:25,293 --> 01:15:29,018
Diastolic pressure decreases more than systolic.

594
01:15:29,018 --> 01:15:32,743
Changes in posture affect arterial blood pressure .

595
01:15:32,743 --> 01:15:38,756
Brachial artery pressure when sitting is lower than that when in the lateral recumbent supine position .

596
01:15:38,756 --> 01:15:46,643
Additionally, systolic blood pressure is lower in the lateral positions compared with either the flexed sitting or supine positions .

597
01:15:46,643 --> 01:15:58,731
In approximately 10 percent of women, supine compression of the great vessels by the uterus causes significant arterial hypotension, sometimes referred to as the supine hypotensive syndrome.

598
01:15:58,731 --> 01:16:07,218
Also when supine, uterine arterial pressure—and thus uterine blood flow—is significantly lower than that in the brachial artery.

599
01:16:07,218 --> 01:16:19,406
Evidence to support whether this directly affects fetal heart rate patterns in uncomplicated low-risk pregnancies is conflicting (Armstrong, 2011; Ibrahim, 2015).

600
01:16:19,406 --> 01:16:24,581
Similar changes can also be seen with hemorrhage or with spinal analgesia.

601
01:16:24,581 --> 01:16:26,993
Figure 4-11.

602
01:16:26,993 --> 01:16:38,831
Sequential changes (± SEM) in blood pressure throughout pregnancy in 69 women in supine (blue lines) and left lateral recumbent positions (red lines).

603
01:16:38,831 --> 01:16:41,406
PP = postpartum.

604
01:16:41,406 --> 01:16:51,743
Studies have assessed vascular compliance before pregnancy, during pregnancy, and postpartum (Morris, 2015; Osman, 2017).

605
01:16:51,743 --> 01:17:02,681
Compared with healthy nonpregnant controls, significant declines in mean arterial pressure and arterial stiffness were observed between prepregnant and postpartum time periods.

606
01:17:02,681 --> 01:17:09,593
These findings suggest that pregnancy confers a favorable effect on maternal cardiovascular remodeling.

607
01:17:09,593 --> 01:17:11,931
The venous system is a

608
01:17:12,100 --> 01:17:16,237
high-flow, low-resistance circulation during pregnancy .

609
01:17:16,187 --> 01:17:30,300
While antecubital venous pressure remains unchanged, in the supine position, femoral venous pressure rises steadily, from approximately 8 mm Hg early in pregnancy to 24 mm Hg at term.

610
01:17:30,300 --> 01:17:36,262
Venous blood flow in the legs is retarded except when the lateral recumbent position is assumed.

611
01:17:36,262 --> 01:17:47,275
This tendency toward blood stagnation in the lower extremities during later pregnancy is attributable to occlusion of the pelvic veins and inferior vena cava by the enlarged uterus.

612
01:17:47,275 --> 01:17:54,425
The elevated venous pressure returns to normal when the pregnant woman lies on her side and immediately after delivery.

613
01:17:54,425 --> 01:18:03,400
These alterations contribute to the dependent edema frequently experienced and to development of hemorrhoids and varicose veins in the legs and vulva.

614
01:18:03,400 --> 01:18:09,175
These changes also predispose to deepvein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.

615
01:18:09,175 --> 01:18:13,712
# Renin, Angiotensin II, and Plasma Volume.

616
01:18:13,712 --> 01:18:21,400
The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis is intimately involved in blood pressure control via sodium and water balance.

617
01:18:21,400 --> 01:18:26,150
All components of this system show increased levels in normal pregnancy.

618
01:18:26,150 --> 01:18:36,762
Renin is produced by both the maternal kidney and the placenta, and greater amounts of renin substrate (angiotensinogen) are produced by both maternal and fetal liver.

619
01:18:36,762 --> 01:18:47,687
Elevated angiotensinogen levels result, in part, from augmented estrogen production during normal pregnancy and are important in first-trimester blood pressure maintenance .

620
01:18:47,687 --> 01:18:58,475
Gant and associates (1973) reported that nulliparas who remained normotensive became and stayed refractory to the pressor effects of infused angiotensin II.

621
01:18:58,475 --> 01:19:05,125
Conversely, those who ultimately became hypertensive developed, but then lost, this refractoriness.

622
01:19:05,125 --> 01:19:11,062
The diminished vascular responsiveness to angiotensin II may be progesterone related.

623
01:19:11,062 --> 01:19:15,962
Placental growth factor (PlGF) also blunts this response .

624
01:19:15,962 --> 01:19:25,550
Normally, pregnant women lose their acquired vascular refractoriness to angiotensin II within 15 to 30 minutes after the placenta is delivered.

625
01:19:25,550 --> 01:19:32,300
Large amounts of intramuscular progesterone given during late labor delay this diminishing responsiveness.

626
01:19:32,300 --> 01:19:35,950
# Cardiac Natriuretic Peptides.

627
01:19:35,950 --> 01:19:48,462
At least two species of these—atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) and brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)—are secreted by cardiomyocytes in response to chamber-wall stretching.

628
01:19:48,462 --> 01:19:56,750
These peptides regulate blood volume by provoking natriuresis, diuresis, and vascular smooth-muscle relaxation.

629
01:19:56,750 --> 01:20:22,062
In nonpregnant and pregnant patients, levels of BNP and of amino-terminal pro– brain natriuretic peptide (Nt pro-BNP), as well as newer analytes such as suppressor of tumorigenicity 2 (ST2), may be useful in screening for depressed left ventricular systolic function and determining chronic heart failure prognosis (Cunningham, 2019; Ker, 2018).

630
01:20:22,062 --> 01:20:30,812
During normal pregnancy, plasma ANP and BNP levels are maintained in the nonpregnant range despite greater plasma volume .

631
01:20:30,812 --> 01:20:38,550
In one study, median BNP levels were stable across pregnancy with values <20 pg/mL .

632
01:20:38,550 --> 01:20:46,662
BNP levels are elevated in severe preeclampsia, and this may be caused by cardiac strain from increased afterload.

633
01:20:46,662 --> 01:20:59,412
It would appear that ANP-induced physiological adaptations participate in extracellular fluid volume expansion and in the elevated plasma aldosterone concentrations characteristic of normal pregnancy.

634
01:20:59,412 --> 01:21:02,037
# Prostaglandins.

635
01:21:02,037 --> 01:21:11,350
Elevated prostaglandin production during pregnancy is thought to have a central role in control of vascular tone, blood pressure, and sodium balance.

636
01:21:11,350 --> 01:21:19,475
Renal medullary prostaglandin E2 synthesis is markedly elevated during late pregnancy and is presumed to be natriuretic.

637
01:21:19,475 --> 01:21:27,825
Levels of prostacyclin (PGI2), the principal prostaglandin of endothelium, also rise during late pregnancy.

638
01:21:27,825 --> 01:21:32,087
PGI2 regulates blood pressure and platelet function.

639
01:21:32,087 --> 01:21:40,112
It helps maintain vasodilation during pregnancy, and its deficiency is associated with pathological vasoconstriction .

640
01:21:40,112 --> 01:21:48,650
The ratio of PGI2 to thromboxane in maternal urine and blood may be important indicators of preeclampsia pathogenesis.

641
01:21:48,650 --> 01:21:50,850
# Endothelin.

642
01:21:50,850 --> 01:21:56,375
Pregnancy generates several endothelins, which are vasoconstricting peptides.

643
01:21:56,375 --> 01:21:58,687
Endothelin-1 is a

644
01:21:58,852 --> 01:22:06,689
potent vasoconstrictor produced in endothelial and vascular smooth muscle cells and regulates local vasomotor tone .

645
01:22:06,639 --> 01:22:12,927
Its production is stimulated by angiotensin II, arginine vasopressin, and thrombin.

646
01:22:12,927 --> 01:22:19,939
Endothelins, in turn, stimulate secretion of ANP, aldosterone, and catecholamines.

647
01:22:19,939 --> 01:22:25,452
Vascular sensitivity to endothelin 1 is not altered during normal pregnancy.

648
01:22:25,452 --> 01:22:30,102
Pathologically elevated levels may play a role in preeclampsia .

649
01:22:30,102 --> 01:22:32,652
# Nitric Oxide.

650
01:22:32,652 --> 01:22:40,152
This potent vasodilator is released by endothelial cells and may modify vascular resistance during pregnancy.

651
01:22:40,152 --> 01:22:48,502
As discussed earlier (p. 52), nitric oxide is an important mediator of placental vascular tone and development .

652
01:22:48,502 --> 01:22:57,577
Abnormal nitric oxide synthesis has been linked to preeclampsia (Laskowska, 2015; Vignini, 2016).

653
01:22:57,577 --> 01:22:59,739
Respiratory Tract.

654
01:22:59,739 --> 01:23:06,289
Of anatomical changes, the diaphragm rises approximately 4 cm during pregnancy .

655
01:23:06,289 --> 01:23:14,627
The subcostal angle widens appreciably as the transverse diameter of the thoracic cage lengthens approximately 2 cm.

656
01:23:14,627 --> 01:23:24,614
The thoracic circumference increases about 6 cm, but not sufficiently to prevent reduced residual lung volumes created by the elevated diaphragm.

657
01:23:24,614 --> 01:23:29,289
Even so, diaphragmatic excursion is greater in pregnancy.

658
01:23:29,289 --> 01:23:31,402
Dyspnea is common .

659
01:23:31,402 --> 01:23:33,777
Figure 4-12.

660
01:23:33,777 --> 01:23:38,902
Chest wall measurements in nonpregnant (left) and pregnant women (right).

661
01:23:38,902 --> 01:23:47,527
The subcostal angle increases, as does the anteroposterior and transverse diameters of the chest wall and chest wall circumference.

662
01:23:47,527 --> 01:23:55,914
These changes compensate for the 4-cm elevation of the diaphragm so that total lung capacity is not significantly reduced.

663
01:23:55,914 --> 01:24:09,202
Redrawn from Hegewald MJ, Crapo RO. Respiratory physiology in pregnancy, Clin Chest Med 2011 Mar;32(1):1–13.) # Pulmonary Function.

664
01:24:09,202 --> 01:24:21,339
Of physiological lung changes, functional residual capacity (FRC) decreases by approximately 20 to 30 percent or 400 to 700 mL during pregnancy .

665
01:24:21,339 --> 01:24:36,352
This capacity is composed of expiratory reserve volume—which drops 15 to 20 percent or 200 to 300 mL—and residual volume—which decreases 20 to 25 percent or 200 to 400 mL.

666
01:24:36,352 --> 01:24:42,789
FRC and residual volume decline progressively across pregnancy due to diaphragm elevation.

667
01:24:42,789 --> 01:24:46,752
Significant reductions are observed by the sixth month.

668
01:24:46,752 --> 01:24:57,752
Inspiratory capacity, the maximum volume that can be inhaled from FRC, rises by 5 to 10 percent or 200 to 350 mL during pregnancy.

669
01:24:57,752 --> 01:25:06,914
Total lung capacity—the combination of FRC and inspiratory capacity—is unchanged or declines by <5 percent at term .

670
01:25:06,914 --> 01:25:09,527
Figure 4-13.

671
01:25:09,527 --> 01:25:12,652
Changes in lung volumes with pregnancy.

672
01:25:12,652 --> 01:25:30,527
The most significant changes are reduction in functional residual capacity (FRC) and its subcomponents, expiratory reserve volume (ERV) and residual volume (RV), as well as increases in inspiratory capacity (IC) and tidal volume (VT).

673
01:25:30,527 --> 01:25:42,627
Reproduced with permission from Hegewald MJ, Crapo RO. Respiratory physiology in pregnancy, Clin Chest Med 2011 Mar;32(1):1–13.

674
01:25:42,627 --> 01:25:51,652
The respiratory rate is essentially unchanged, but tidal volume and resting minute ventilation increase significantly as pregnancy advances.

675
01:25:51,652 --> 01:26:09,177
Kolarzyk and coworkers (2005) reported significantly greater mean tidal volumes—0.66 to 0.8 L/min—and resting minute ventilations—10.7 to 14.1 L/min—compared with those of nonpregnant women.

676
01:26:09,177 --> 01:26:13,477
The elevated minute ventilation is caused by several factors.

677
01:26:13,477 --> 01:26:24,927
These include enhanced respiratory drive primarily due to the stimulatory action of progesterone, low expiratory reserve volume, and compensated respiratory alkalosis .

678
01:26:24,927 --> 01:26:30,589
Also, decreased plasma osmolality can result in less respiratory depression .

679
01:26:30,589 --> 01:26:37,939
Regarding pulmonary function, peak expiratory flow rates rise progressively as gestation advances .

680
01:26:37,939 --> 01:26:41,402
Lung compliance is unaffected by pregnancy.

681
01:26:41,402 --> 01:26:48,589
Airway conductance is increased and total pulmonary resistance reduced, possibly as a result of progesterone.

682
01:26:48,589 --> 01:26:54,777
The maximum breathing capacity and forced or timed vital capacity are not altered appreciably.

683
01:26:54,777 --> 01:27:05,177
It is unclear whether the critical closing volume—the lung volume at which airways in the dependent parts of the lung begin to close during expiration—is higher in pregnancy .

684
01:27:05,177 --> 01:27:10,627
Pulmonary function with a singleton pregnancy does not significantly differ from that

685
01:27:10,780 --> 01:27:12,505
with twins .

686
01:27:12,455 --> 01:27:22,455
Importantly, the greater oxygen requirements and perhaps the increased critical closing volume imposed by pregnancy make respiratory diseases more serious.

687
01:27:22,455 --> 01:27:29,292
Demir and colleagues (2015) studied nasal physiology in 85 pregnant women.

688
01:27:29,292 --> 01:27:43,467
Although the minimal cross-sectional area decreased between the first and third trimesters, subjective reports of nasal congestion or total nasal resistance did not significantly differ among trimesters or compared with nonpregnant controls.

689
01:27:43,467 --> 01:27:46,030
# Oxygen Delivery.

690
01:27:46,030 --> 01:27:54,592
The amount of oxygen delivered into the lungs by the increased tidal volume clearly exceeds oxygen requirements imposed by pregnancy.

691
01:27:54,592 --> 01:28:05,430
Moreover, the total hemoglobin mass and, in turn, total oxygen-carrying capacity rise appreciably during normal pregnancy, as does cardiac output.

692
01:28:05,430 --> 01:28:10,667
Consequently, the maternal arteriovenous oxygen difference is diminished.

693
01:28:10,667 --> 01:28:19,930
Oxygen consumption grows approximately 20 percent during pregnancy, and it is approximately 10 percent higher in multifetal gestations .

694
01:28:19,930 --> 01:28:25,067
During labor, oxygen consumption increases 40 to 60 percent .

695
01:28:25,067 --> 01:28:28,105
# Acid–Base Equilibrium.

696
01:28:28,105 --> 01:28:33,192
A greater awareness of a desire to breathe is common even early in pregnancy .

697
01:28:33,192 --> 01:28:40,605
This may be interpreted as dyspnea, which may suggest pulmonary or cardiac abnormalities when none exist.

698
01:28:40,605 --> 01:28:57,167
This physiological dyspnea, which should not interfere with normal physical activity, is thought to result from greater tidal volume that lowers the partial pressure of carbon dioxide (CO2) in blood (PaCO2) slightly and paradoxically causes dyspnea.

699
01:28:57,167 --> 01:29:08,267
The increased respiratory effort during pregnancy, and in turn the reduction in the PaCO2, is likely induced in large part by progesterone and to a lesser degree by estrogen.

700
01:29:08,267 --> 01:29:10,892
Progesterone acts centrally.

701
01:29:10,892 --> 01:29:17,892
Here, it lowers the threshold and raises the sensitivity of the chemoreflex response to carbon dioxide .

702
01:29:17,892 --> 01:29:27,230
To compensate for the resulting respiratory alkalosis, plasma bicarbonate levels normally drop from 26 to 22 mmol/L.

703
01:29:27,230 --> 01:29:34,017
Although blood pH is increased only minimally, it does shift the oxygen dissociation curve to the left.

704
01:29:34,017 --> 01:29:43,830
This shift increases the affinity of maternal hemoglobin for oxygen—the Bohr effect—thereby lowering the oxygen-releasing capacity of maternal blood.

705
01:29:43,830 --> 01:29:52,305
This is offset because the slight pH rise also stimulates an increase in 2,3diphosphoglycerate in maternal erythrocytes.

706
01:29:52,305 --> 01:29:55,217
This shifts the curve back to the right .

707
01:29:55,217 --> 01:30:05,755
Thus, reduced PaCO2 from maternal hyperventilation aids CO2 (waste) transfer from the fetus to the mother while also aiding oxygen release to the fetus.

708
01:30:05,755 --> 01:30:07,792
Urinary System.

709
01:30:07,792 --> 01:30:09,805
# Kidney.

710
01:30:09,805 --> 01:30:14,780
The urinary system undergoes several remarkable changes in pregnancy .

711
01:30:14,780 --> 01:30:18,442
Kidney size grows approximately 1.0 cm .

712
01:30:18,442 --> 01:30:25,342
Both the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and renal plasma flow increase early in pregnancy.

713
01:30:25,342 --> 01:30:33,842
The GFR rises as much as 25 percent by the second week after conception and 50 percent by the beginning of the second trimester .

714
01:30:33,842 --> 01:30:37,942
This hyperfiltration results from two principal factors.

715
01:30:37,942 --> 01:30:47,617
First, hypervolemia-induced hemodilution lowers the protein concentration and oncotic pressure of plasma entering the glomerular microcirculation.

716
01:30:47,617 --> 01:30:59,230
Second, renal plasma flow rises by approximately 80 percent before the end of the first trimester (Conrad, 2015; Lopes van Balen, 2019).

717
01:30:59,230 --> 01:31:06,167
Elevated GFR persists until term, even though renal plasma flow declines during late pregnancy.

718
01:31:06,167 --> 01:31:18,630
Primarily as a consequence of this elevated GFR, approximately 60 percent of nulliparas during the third trimester experience urinary frequency, and 80 percent experience nocturia .

719
01:31:18,630 --> 01:31:21,042
Table 4-6.

720
01:31:21,042 --> 01:31:25,480
Renal Changes in Normal Pregnancy Figure 4-14.

721
01:31:25,480 --> 01:31:30,692
Glomerular filtration rate across pregnancy as measured by inulin clearance.

722
01:31:30,692 --> 01:31:37,180
The solid line is the 50th percentile and the dashed lines are the 5th and 95th percentiles.

723
01:31:37,180 --> 01:31:46,605
During the puerperium, a marked GFR persists during the first postpartum day, principally from the reduced glomerular capillary oncotic pressure.

724
01:31:46,605 --> 01:31:55,005
The gestational hypervolemia and hemodilution, still evident on the first postpartum day, resolves by the second week postpartum.

725
01:31:55,005 --> 01:32:02,580
Studies suggest that relaxin, discussed earlier (p. 54), may mediate both increased GFR

726
01:32:02,740 --> 01:32:05,702
and renal blood flow during pregnancy .

727
01:32:05,652 --> 01:32:15,240
Relaxin boosts renal nitric oxide production, which leads to renal vasodilation and lowered renal afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance.

728
01:32:15,240 --> 01:32:18,715
This augments renal blood flow and GFR .

729
01:32:18,715 --> 01:32:31,865
Relaxin may also increase vascular gelatinase activity during pregnancy, which leads to renal vasodilation, glomerular hyperfiltration, and reduced myogenic reactivity of small renal arteries .

730
01:32:31,865 --> 01:32:38,565
As with blood pressure, maternal posture may considerably influence several aspects of renal function.

731
01:32:38,565 --> 01:32:46,702
Late in pregnancy, the sodium excretion rate in the supine position averages less than half that in the lateral recumbent position.

732
01:32:46,702 --> 01:32:51,240
The effects of posture on GFR and renal plasma flow vary.

733
01:32:51,240 --> 01:32:59,790
One unusual feature of the pregnancy-induced changes in renal excretion is the remarkably higher amounts of some nutrients lost in the urine.

734
01:32:59,790 --> 01:33:05,327
Amino acids and water-soluble vitamins are excreted in much greater amounts .

735
01:33:05,327 --> 01:33:08,115
# Renal Function Tests.

736
01:33:08,115 --> 01:33:18,677
The mean nonpregnancy serum creatinine level of 0.7 mg/dL declines during normal pregnancy to 0.5 mg/dL.

737
01:33:18,677 --> 01:33:27,715
Values ≥0.9 mg/dL suggest underlying renal disease and prompt further evaluation.

738
01:33:27,715 --> 01:33:36,540
Creatinine clearance in pregnancy averages 30 percent higher than the 100 to 115 mL/min in nonpregnant women.

739
01:33:36,540 --> 01:33:44,927
This is a useful test to estimate renal function, provided that complete urine collection is made during an accurately timed period.

740
01:33:44,927 --> 01:33:48,890
If this is not done precisely, results are misleading .

741
01:33:48,890 --> 01:33:54,377
During the day, pregnant women tend to accumulate water as dependent edema.

742
01:33:54,377 --> 01:33:59,002
When recumbent at night, they mobilize this fluid with diuresis.

743
01:33:59,002 --> 01:34:08,190
This reversal of the usual nonpregnant diurnal pattern of urinary flow causes nocturia, and urine is more dilute than in nonpregnant women.

744
01:34:08,190 --> 01:34:18,002
Failure of a pregnant woman to excrete concentrated urine after withholding fluids for approximately 18 hours does not necessarily signify renal damage.

745
01:34:18,002 --> 01:34:27,465
In fact, the kidneys in these circumstances function perfectly normally by excreting mobilized extracellular fluid of relatively low osmolality.

746
01:34:27,465 --> 01:34:29,927
# Urinalysis.

747
01:34:29,927 --> 01:34:33,727
Glucosuria during pregnancy may not be abnormal.

748
01:34:33,727 --> 01:34:43,615
The appreciably increased GFR, together with impaired tubular reabsorptive capacity for filtered glucose, accounts for most cases of glucosuria.

749
01:34:43,615 --> 01:34:51,177
Chesley (1963) calculated that approximately one sixth of gravidas will spill glucose in the urine.

750
01:34:51,177 --> 01:35:01,865
As discussed in Chapter 56 (p. 994), although the sensitivity is low, glucosuria may be an indicator of gestational diabetes mellitus.

751
01:35:01,865 --> 01:35:16,052
For women with glucosuria, no guidelines in the United States direct practice, but in the United Kingdom, early oral glucose testing is considered for those with an isolated 2+ or repetitive 1+ urine dipstick readings .

752
01:35:16,052 --> 01:35:21,202
Hematuria most often suggests urinary tract disease or infection.

753
01:35:21,202 --> 01:35:27,390
Evaluation of this finding is outlined in Chapter 56 (p. 995).

754
01:35:27,390 --> 01:35:33,977
Hematuria is common after difficult labor and delivery because of trauma to the bladder and urethra.

755
01:35:33,977 --> 01:35:42,165
Proteinuria is typically defined in nonpregnant subjects as a protein excretion rate of more than 150 mg/d.

756
01:35:42,165 --> 01:35:56,415
Because of the aforementioned hyperfiltration and possible reduction of tubular reabsorption, proteinuria during pregnancy is usually considered significant once a protein excretion threshold of at least 300 mg/d is reached .

757
01:35:56,415 --> 01:36:04,427
Higby and coworkers (1994) measured protein excretion in 270 normal women throughout pregnancy .

758
01:36:04,427 --> 01:36:18,127
Mean 24-hour excretion for all three trimesters was 115 mg, and the upper 95-percent confidence limit was 260 mg/d without significant differences by trimester.

759
01:36:18,127 --> 01:36:29,615
They showed that albumin excretion is minimal and ranges from 5 to 30 mg/d. Proteinuria increases with gestational age, which corresponds with the peak in GFR .

760
01:36:29,615 --> 01:36:32,140
Figure 4-15.

761
01:36:32,140 --> 01:36:38,777
Scatter plot of women showing 24-hour urinary total protein excretion by gestational age.

762
01:36:38,777 --> 01:36:42,790
Mean and 95-percent confidence limits are outlined.

763
01:36:42,790 --> 01:37:01,402
Modified with permission from Higby K, Suiter CR, Phelps JY, et al: Normal values of urinary albumin and total protein excretion during pregnancy, Am J Obstet Gynecol 1994 Oct;171(4):984–989.

764
01:37:01,402 --> 01:37:06,165
Measuring Urine Protein The three most commonly employed approaches for

765
01:37:06,316 --> 01:37:19,378
assessing proteinuria are the qualitative classic dipstick, the quantitative 24-hour collection, and the albumin/creatinine or protein/creatinine ratio of a single voided urine specimen.

766
01:37:19,328 --> 01:37:26,616
The pitfalls of each approach have been reviewed (Bramham, 2016; Conrad, 2015).

767
01:37:26,616 --> 01:37:34,016
With dipstick assessment, the principal problem is its failure to account for renal concentration or dilution of urine.

768
01:37:34,016 --> 01:37:43,716
For example, with polyuria and extremely dilute urine, a negative or trace dipstick could actually be associated with excessive protein excretion.

769
01:37:43,716 --> 01:37:50,641
The 24-hour urine collection is affected by urinary tract dilation, discussed in the next section.

770
01:37:50,641 --> 01:38:03,253
The dilated tract may lead to errors related both to retention—hundreds of milliliters of urine remaining in the dilated tract—and to timing—the remaining urine may have formed hours before the collection.

771
01:38:03,253 --> 01:38:14,003
To minimize these pitfalls, the patient is first hydrated and positioned in lateral recumbency—the definitive nonobstructive posture—for 45 to 60 minutes.

772
01:38:14,003 --> 01:38:18,791
After this, she is asked to void, and this specimen is discarded.

773
01:38:18,791 --> 01:38:23,803
Immediately following this void, her 24-hour collection begins.

774
01:38:23,803 --> 01:38:29,903
During the final hour of collection, the patient is again placed in the lateral recumbent position.

775
01:38:29,903 --> 01:38:36,653
But, at the end of this hour, the final collected urine is incorporated into the total collected volume .

776
01:38:36,653 --> 01:38:44,841
Last, the protein:creatinine ratio is a promising approach because data can be obtained quickly and collection errors are avoided.

777
01:38:44,841 --> 01:38:55,578
Disadvantageously, the amount of protein per unit of creatinine excreted during a 24-hour period is not constant, and the thresholds to define abnormal vary.

778
01:38:55,578 --> 01:39:03,016
Nomograms for urinary microalbumin and creatinine ratios during uncomplicated pregnancies have been developed.

779
01:39:03,016 --> 01:39:06,228
The ratio is unreliable postpartum .

780
01:39:06,228 --> 01:39:08,391
# Ureters.

781
01:39:08,391 --> 01:39:13,828
After the uterus completely rises out of the pelvis, it rests on the ureters.

782
01:39:13,828 --> 01:39:18,228
This laterally displaces and compresses them at the pelvic brim.

783
01:39:18,228 --> 01:39:27,128
Above this level, elevated intraureteral tonus results, and ureteral dilation (hydroureter) and hydronephrosis is impressive.

784
01:39:27,128 --> 01:39:31,766
It is greater on the right side as shown in Figure 4-16.

785
01:39:31,766 --> 01:39:35,353
It is right sided in 86 percent of women.

786
01:39:35,353 --> 01:39:46,378
This unequal dilation may result from cushioning provided to the left ureter by the sigmoid colon and perhaps from greater right ureteral compression exerted by the dextrorotated uterus.

787
01:39:46,378 --> 01:39:58,028
The right ovarian vein complex, which is remarkably dilated during pregnancy, lies obliquely over the right ureter and may also contribute to ureteral compression and proximal dilation.

788
01:39:58,028 --> 01:40:00,591
Figure 4-16.

789
01:40:00,591 --> 01:40:02,653
Hydronephrosis.

790
01:40:02,653 --> 01:40:07,991
Plain film from the 15-minute image of an intravenous pyelogram (IVP).

791
01:40:07,991 --> 01:40:17,703
Moderate hydronephrosis on the right (arrows) and mild hydronephrosis on the left (arrowheads) are both normal for this 35-week gestation.

792
01:40:17,703 --> 01:40:21,091
Progesterone likely has some additional effect.

793
01:40:21,091 --> 01:40:31,066
Van Wagenen and Jenkins (1939) described continued ureteral dilation after removal of the monkey fetus but with the placenta left in situ.

794
01:40:31,066 --> 01:40:39,141
The relatively abrupt onset of dilation in women at midpregnancy, however, seems more consistent with ureteral compression.

795
01:40:39,141 --> 01:40:49,353
Ureteral elongation accompanies distention, and the ureter is frequently thrown into curves of varying size, and smaller curves may be sharply angulated.

796
01:40:49,353 --> 01:40:54,441
These so-called kinks are poorly named, because the term connotes obstruction.

797
01:40:54,441 --> 01:41:03,528
They are usually single or double curves that, when viewed in a radiograph taken in the same plane as the curve, may appear as acute angulations.

798
01:41:03,528 --> 01:41:09,066
Another exposure at right angles nearly always identifies them to be gentle curves.

799
01:41:09,066 --> 01:41:18,828
Despite these anatomical changes, complication rates associated with ureteroscopy in pregnant and nonpregnant patients do not differ significantly .

800
01:41:18,828 --> 01:41:20,803
# Bladder.

801
01:41:20,803 --> 01:41:26,503
The bladder shows few significant anatomical changes before 12 weeks’ gestation.

802
01:41:26,503 --> 01:41:40,203
Subsequently, however, increased uterine size, the hyperemia that affects all pelvic organs, and hyperplasia of bladder muscle and connective tissues together elevate the trigone and thicken its intraureteric margin.

803
01:41:40,203 --> 01:41:45,566
Continuation of this process to term produces marked deepening and widening of the

804
01:41:45,724 --> 01:41:47,311
trigone.

805
01:41:47,261 --> 01:41:53,911
The bladder mucosa is unchanged other than an increase in the size and tortuosity of its blood vessels.

806
01:41:53,911 --> 01:42:02,636
Bladder pressure in primigravidas rises from 8 cm H2O early in pregnancy to 20 cm H2O at term .

807
01:42:02,636 --> 01:42:12,886
To compensate for reduced bladder capacity, absolute and functional urethral lengths increase by 6.7 and 4.8 mm, respectively.

808
01:42:12,886 --> 01:42:21,911
Concurrently, maximal intraurethral pressure rises from 70 to 93 cm H2O, and thus continence is maintained.

809
01:42:21,911 --> 01:42:28,436
Still, at least half of women experience some degree of urinary incontinence by the third trimester .

810
01:42:28,436 --> 01:42:34,161
Indeed, this is always considered in the differential diagnosis of ruptured membranes.

811
01:42:34,161 --> 01:42:44,186
Near term—particularly in nulliparas, in whom the presenting part often engages before labor—the entire base of the bladder is pushed ventral and cephalad.

812
01:42:44,186 --> 01:42:48,574
This converts the normally convex surface into a concavity.

813
01:42:48,574 --> 01:42:53,524
As a result, diagnostic and therapeutic procedures are more difficult.

814
01:42:53,524 --> 01:43:05,674
Moreover, pressure from the presenting part impairs blood and lymph drainage from the bladder base, often rendering the area edematous, easily traumatized, and possibly more susceptible to infection.

815
01:43:05,674 --> 01:43:08,124
Gastrointestinal Tract.

816
01:43:08,124 --> 01:43:14,886
As pregnancy progresses, the stomach and intestines are displaced cephalad by the enlarging uterus.

817
01:43:14,886 --> 01:43:19,649
Consequently, the physical findings in certain diseases are altered.

818
01:43:19,649 --> 01:43:25,099
The appendix, for instance, is usually displaced upward and somewhat laterally.

819
01:43:25,099 --> 01:43:28,449
At times, it may reach the right flank.

820
01:43:28,449 --> 01:43:37,261
Pyrosis (heartburn) is common during pregnancy and is most likely caused by reflux of acidic secretions into the lower esophagus.

821
01:43:37,261 --> 01:43:45,186
Although the altered stomach position probably contributes to its frequency, lower esophageal sphincter tone also is diminished.

822
01:43:45,186 --> 01:43:51,799
In addition, intraesophageal pressures are lower and intragastric pressures higher in pregnant women.

823
01:43:51,799 --> 01:43:57,661
Concurrently, esophageal peristalsis has lower wave speed and lower amplitude .

824
01:43:57,661 --> 01:44:04,586
Gastric emptying time is essentially unchanged across pregnancy in gravidas compared with nonpregnant women.

825
01:44:04,586 --> 01:44:10,886
In fasting women at term, gastric volume is not different than that in nonpregnant controls .

826
01:44:10,886 --> 01:44:20,111
At term, during labor, and especially after administration of analgesics, however, gastric emptying time may be appreciably prolonged .

827
01:44:20,111 --> 01:44:29,874
As a result, one danger of general anesthesia for delivery is regurgitation and aspiration of either food-laden or highly acidic gastric contents.

828
01:44:29,874 --> 01:44:34,811
Some reports describe maternal gut microbiome changes during pregnancy .

829
01:44:34,811 --> 01:44:42,036
Currently, however, the vaginal and gut microbiome profiles and their influence on pregnancy are unclear .

830
01:44:42,036 --> 01:44:45,011
Hemorrhoids are common during pregnancy .

831
01:44:45,011 --> 01:44:52,724
They are caused in large measure by constipation and elevated pressure in rectal veins below the level of the enlarged uterus.

832
01:44:52,724 --> 01:44:54,674
# Liver.

833
01:44:54,674 --> 01:44:58,599
Liver size does not enlarge during human pregnancy.

834
01:44:58,599 --> 01:45:04,299
Hepatic arterial and portal venous blood flow, however, rise substantively .

835
01:45:04,299 --> 01:45:10,636
Liver stiffness increases from the second to third trimester and returns to baseline postpartum .

836
01:45:10,636 --> 01:45:18,336
Some laboratory test results of hepatic function are altered in normal pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1229).

837
01:45:18,336 --> 01:45:27,674
Total alkaline phosphatase activity almost doubles, but much of the rise is attributable to heat-stable placental alkaline phosphatase isozymes.

838
01:45:27,674 --> 01:45:40,549
Serum aspartate transaminase (AST), alanine transaminase (ALT), γ-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT), and bilirubin levels are slightly lower compared with nonpregnant values .

839
01:45:40,549 --> 01:45:44,936
The serum albumin concentration declines during pregnancy.

840
01:45:44,936 --> 01:45:55,474
By late pregnancy, albumin levels may be near 3.0 g/dL compared with approximately 4.3 g/dL in nonpregnant women .

841
01:45:55,474 --> 01:46:02,949
Total body albumin levels rise, however, because of pregnancy-associated expansion of plasma volume.

842
01:46:02,949 --> 01:46:06,586
Serum globulin levels also are slightly higher.

843
01:46:06,586 --> 01:46:13,549
Leucine aminopeptidase is a proteolytic liver enzyme whose serum levels may be elevated with liver disease.

844
01:46:13,549 --> 01:46:17,249
Its activity is markedly higher in pregnant women.

845
01:46:17,249 --> 01:46:24,174
The rise, however, results from a pregnancy-specific enzyme(s) with distinct substrate specificities .

846
01:46:24,174 --> 01:46:33,349
Pregnancyinduced aminopeptidase has oxytocinase and vasopressinase activity that occasionally causes transient diabetes insipidus .

847
01:46:33,349 --> 01:46:35,049
#

848
01:46:35,212 --> 01:46:36,899
Gallbladder.

849
01:46:36,849 --> 01:46:47,099
During normal pregnancy, gallbladder volume expands by approximately 50 percent, contractility declines, and both lead to greater residual volume .

850
01:46:47,099 --> 01:46:58,224
Progesterone potentially impairs gallbladder contraction by inhibiting cholecystokinin-mediated smooth muscle stimulation, which is the primary regulator of gallbladder contraction.

851
01:46:58,224 --> 01:47:09,024
Impaired emptying, subsequent stasis, and the greater cholesterol saturation of bile in pregnancy contribute to the increased prevalence of cholesterol gallstones in multiparas.

852
01:47:09,024 --> 01:47:14,924
In one study, approximately 8 percent of women had gallbladder sludge or stones .

853
01:47:14,924 --> 01:47:21,824
The pregnancy effects on maternal serum bile acid concentrations are still incompletely characterized.

854
01:47:21,824 --> 01:47:29,487
This is despite the long-acknowledged propensity for pregnancy to cause intrahepatic cholestasis from retained bile salts.

855
01:47:29,487 --> 01:47:35,362
Cholestasis of pregnancy is described in Chapter 58 (p. 1032).

856
01:47:35,362 --> 01:47:37,437
Endocrine System.

857
01:47:37,437 --> 01:47:40,112
# Pituitary Gland.

858
01:47:40,112 --> 01:47:47,087
During normal pregnancy, the pituitary gland enlarges by approximately 135 percent .

859
01:47:47,087 --> 01:47:52,912
This expansion may sufficiently compress the optic chiasma to reduce visual fields.

860
01:47:52,912 --> 01:47:57,537
Impaired vision from this is rare and usually due to macroadenomas .

861
01:47:57,537 --> 01:48:05,274
Pituitary enlargement is primarily caused by estrogen-stimulated hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the lactotrophs .

862
01:48:05,274 --> 01:48:11,074
As discussed subsequently, maternal serum prolactin levels parallel this growth.

863
01:48:11,074 --> 01:48:17,549
Gonadotroph number decline, and corticotroph and thyrotroph populations remain constant.

864
01:48:17,549 --> 01:48:24,399
Somatotroph function is generally suppressed due to negative feedback by the placental production of growth hormone.

865
01:48:24,399 --> 01:48:30,837
Peak pituitary size may reach 12 mm in MR images in the first days postpartum.

866
01:48:30,837 --> 01:48:36,437
The gland involutes rapidly and reaches normal size by 6 months postpartum .

867
01:48:36,437 --> 01:48:41,749
The incidence of pituitary prolactinomas is not increased during pregnancy.

868
01:48:41,749 --> 01:48:53,137
When these tumors are large before pregnancy, such as with macroadenomas that by definition measure ≥10 mm, then growth during pregnancy is more likely .

869
01:48:53,137 --> 01:48:57,949
The maternal pituitary gland is not essential for pregnancy maintenance.

870
01:48:57,949 --> 01:49:10,312
Many women have undergone hypophysectomy, completed pregnancy successfully, and entered spontaneous labor while receiving compensatory glucocorticoids, thyroid hormone, and vasopressin.

871
01:49:10,312 --> 01:49:23,687
Of pituitary gland hormones, growth hormone during the first trimester is secreted predominantly from the maternal pituitary gland, and concentrations in serum and amnionic fluid lie within the nonpregnant range.

872
01:49:23,687 --> 01:49:35,499
As early as 6 weeks’ gestation, growth hormone secreted from the placenta becomes detectable, and by approximately 20 weeks, the placenta is the principal source of growth hormone secretion .

873
01:49:35,499 --> 01:49:48,824
During this time, maternal serum values rise slowly from approximately 3.5 ng/mL at 10 weeks to plateau at about 14 ng/mL after 28 weeks’ gestation.

874
01:49:48,824 --> 01:49:58,924
Growth hormone in amnionic fluid peaks at 14 to 15 weeks’ gestation and slowly declines thereafter to reach baseline values after 36 weeks.

875
01:49:58,924 --> 01:50:08,199
Placental growth hormone—which differs from pituitary growth hormone by 13 amino acid residues—is secreted by syncytiotrophoblast .

876
01:50:08,199 --> 01:50:19,299
Its regulation and physiological effects are incompletely understood, but it influences fetal growth via upregulation of insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1).

877
01:50:19,299 --> 01:50:24,587
That said, fetal growth still progresses in the complete absence of this hormone.

878
01:50:24,587 --> 01:50:32,087
Although not absolutely essential, the hormone may act in concert with placental lactogen to regulate fetal growth .

879
01:50:32,087 --> 01:50:37,399
Prolactin levels rise markedly in maternal plasma during normal pregnancy.

880
01:50:37,399 --> 01:50:47,012
Concentrations are usually tenfold greater at term—approximately 150 ng/mL—compared with those of nonpregnant women.

881
01:50:47,012 --> 01:50:52,887
Paradoxically, plasma levels drop after delivery even in women who are breastfeeding.

882
01:50:52,887 --> 01:50:59,049
During early lactation, pulsatile bursts of prolactin secretion are a response to suckling.

883
01:50:59,049 --> 01:51:03,762
The principal function of maternal prolactin is to ensure lactation.

884
01:51:03,762 --> 01:51:14,199
Early in pregnancy, prolactin acts to initiate DNA synthesis and mitosis of glandular epithelial cells and presecretory alveolar cells of the breast.

885
01:51:14,199 --> 01:51:19,874
Prolactin also augments the number of estrogen and prolactin receptors in these cells.

886
01:51:19,874 --> 01:51:21,399
Last,

887
01:51:21,556 --> 01:51:31,431
prolactin promotes mammary alveolar cell RNA synthesis, galactopoiesis, and production of casein, lactalbumin, lactose, and lipids .

888
01:51:31,381 --> 01:51:37,106
A woman with isolated prolactin deficiency failed to lactate after two pregnancies .

889
01:51:37,106 --> 01:51:42,506
This establishes prolactin as a requisite for lactation but not for pregnancy.

890
01:51:42,506 --> 01:51:51,118
Grattan (2015) has reviewed the numerous physiological roles of prolactin to aid maternal adaptations to pregnancy.

891
01:51:51,118 --> 01:51:59,768
The prolactin receptor in the maternal pancreas indicates that prolactin may function to mediate pancreatic adaptations to pregnancy .

892
01:51:59,768 --> 01:52:10,593
A possible role is proposed for a prolactin fragment in the genesis of peripartum cardiomyopathy (Cunningham, 2019; Koenig, 2018).

893
01:52:10,593 --> 01:52:15,306
Prolactin is present in amnionic fluid in high concentrations.

894
01:52:15,306 --> 01:52:22,031
Levels of up to 10,000 ng/mL are found at 20 to 26 weeks’ gestation.

895
01:52:22,031 --> 01:52:27,218
Thereafter, levels decline and reach a nadir after 34 weeks.

896
01:52:27,218 --> 01:52:32,193
Uterine decidua is the synthesis site of amnionic fluid prolactin.

897
01:52:32,193 --> 01:52:41,843
The exact function of this hormone is unknown, but water transfer from the fetus into the maternal compartment to prevent fetal dehydration is one suggestion.

898
01:52:41,843 --> 01:52:48,431
Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone are hormones secreted from the posterior pituitary gland.

899
01:52:48,431 --> 01:52:59,118
The roles of oxytocin in parturition and lactation are discussed in Chapters 21 (p. 413) and 36 (p. 640), respectively.

900
01:52:59,118 --> 01:53:08,056
Brown and colleagues (2013) have reviewed the complex mechanisms that promote quiescence of oxytocin systems during pregnancy.

901
01:53:08,056 --> 01:53:14,706
Levels of antidiuretic hormone, also called vasopressin, do not change during pregnancy.

902
01:53:14,706 --> 01:53:25,081
As discussed earlier (p. 56), osmolality is slightly higher in maternal compared with fetal blood, and the gradient favors water transport to the fetus .

903
01:53:25,081 --> 01:53:27,593
# Thyroid Gland.

904
01:53:27,593 --> 01:53:32,206
The maternal and fetal thyroid glands are intricately interrelated.

905
01:53:32,206 --> 01:53:47,356
On the maternal side, thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) is secreted by the hypothalamus and stimulates thyrotrope cells of the anterior pituitary to release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), also called thyrotropin.

906
01:53:47,356 --> 01:53:51,381
TRH levels do not rise during normal pregnancy.

907
01:53:51,381 --> 01:53:59,068
However, TRH does cross the placenta and may serve to stimulate the fetal pituitary to secrete TSH.

908
01:53:59,068 --> 01:54:04,031
Serum TSH and hCG levels vary with gestational age .

909
01:54:04,031 --> 01:54:16,131
As discussed in Chapter 5 (p. 96), the α-subunits of the two glycoproteins are identical, whereas the β-subunits, although similar, differ in their amino acid sequence.

910
01:54:16,131 --> 01:54:27,068
As a result of this structural similarity, hCG has intrinsic thyrotropic activity, and thus, high serum hCG levels cause thyroid stimulation.

911
01:54:27,068 --> 01:54:37,543
Indeed, TSH levels in the first trimester decline in more than 80 percent of pregnant women, however, they still remain in the normal range for nonpregnant women.

912
01:54:37,543 --> 01:54:40,181
Figure 4-17.

913
01:54:40,181 --> 01:54:45,406
Relative changes in maternal and fetal thyroid function across pregnancy.

914
01:54:45,406 --> 01:54:57,406
Maternal changes include a marked and early increase in hepatic production of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) and placental production of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG).

915
01:54:57,406 --> 01:55:03,368
Elevated TBG increases serum thyroxine (T4) concentrations.

916
01:55:03,368 --> 01:55:09,618
hCG has thyrotropin-like activity and stimulates maternal free T4 secretion.

917
01:55:09,618 --> 01:55:16,956
This transient hCG-induced increase in serum T4 levels inhibits maternal secretion of thyrotropin.

918
01:55:16,956 --> 01:55:24,343
Except for minimally increased free T4 levels when hCG peaks, these levels are essentially unchanged.

919
01:55:24,343 --> 01:55:30,331
Fetal levels of all serum thyroid analytes increase incrementally across pregnancy.

920
01:55:30,331 --> 01:55:36,031
Fetal triiodothyronine (T3) does not increase until late pregnancy.

921
01:55:36,031 --> 01:55:43,481
The thyroid gland boosts production of thyroid hormones by 40 to 100 percent to meet maternal and fetal needs .

922
01:55:43,481 --> 01:55:52,781
To accomplish this, the thyroid gland undergoes moderate enlargement during pregnancy caused by glandular hyperplasia and greater vascularity.

923
01:55:52,781 --> 01:55:59,718
Mean thyroid volume expands from 12 mL in the first trimester to 15 mL at term .

924
01:55:59,718 --> 01:56:07,756
That said, normal pregnancy does not typically cause significant thyromegaly, and thus any goiter warrants evaluation.

925
01:56:07,756 --> 01:56:15,843
Early in the first trimester, levels of the principal carrier protein— thyroid-binding globulin (TBG)—rise.

926
01:56:15,843 --> 01:56:19,581
They reach their zenith at about 20 weeks’ gestation.

927
01:56:19,581 --> 01:56:23,343
Concentrations stabilize at approximately double

928
01:56:23,500 --> 01:56:28,962
baseline values for the remainder of pregnancy (see Fig. 4-17).

929
01:56:28,912 --> 01:56:41,637
The greater TBG concentrations result from both higher hepatic synthesis rates—due to estrogen stimulation—and lower metabolism rates due to increased TBG sialylation and glycosylation.

930
01:56:41,637 --> 01:56:55,325
These elevated TBG levels increase total serum T4 and triiodothyronine (T3) concentrations, but do not affect the physiologically important serum free T4 and free T3 levels.

931
01:56:55,325 --> 01:57:04,662
Specifically, total serum T4 levels rise sharply beginning between 6 and 9 weeks’ gestation and reach a plateau at 18 weeks.

932
01:57:04,662 --> 01:57:12,275
Serum free T4 levels rise only slightly and peak along with hCG levels, and then they return to normal.

933
01:57:12,275 --> 01:57:18,187
Interestingly, T4 and T3 secretion is not similar for all pregnant women.

934
01:57:18,187 --> 01:57:28,862
Approximately a third of women experience relative hypothyroxinemia, preferential T3 secretion, and higher, albeit normal, serum TSH levels .

935
01:57:28,862 --> 01:57:34,212
Thus, thyroidal adjustments during normal pregnancy may vary considerably.

936
01:57:34,212 --> 01:57:42,525
The fetus relies on maternal T4, which crosses the placenta in small quantities to maintain normal fetal thyroid function .

937
01:57:42,525 --> 01:57:49,212
Recall that the fetal thyroid does not begin to concentrate iodine until 10 to 12 weeks’ gestation.

938
01:57:49,212 --> 01:57:56,875
The synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormone by fetal pituitary TSH ensues at approximately 20 weeks.

939
01:57:56,875 --> 01:58:03,575
At birth, approximately 30 percent of the T4 in umbilical cord blood is of maternal origin .

940
01:58:03,575 --> 01:58:17,725
Thyroid Function Tests As detailed in Chapter 61 (p. 1095), a substantial proportion of otherwise normal pregnancies are found to have subclinical hypothyroidism or isolated hypothyroxinemia.

941
01:58:17,725 --> 01:58:22,787
The effects of thyroxine replacement in these women is currently controversial .

942
01:58:22,787 --> 01:58:30,037
Normal suppression of TSH during pregnancy may lead to a misdiagnosis of subclinical hyperthyroidism.

943
01:58:30,037 --> 01:58:38,662
Of greater concern is the potential failure to identify women with early hypothyroidism because of suppressed TSH concentrations.

944
01:58:38,662 --> 01:58:51,775
To mitigate the likelihood of such misdiagnoses, Dashe and coworkers (2005), and others, have developed gestational-age-specific TSH normal curves for both singleton and twin pregnancies .

945
01:58:51,775 --> 01:58:54,300
Figure 4-18.

946
01:58:54,300 --> 01:59:04,362
Gestational age-specific thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) nomogram derived from 13,599 singleton pregnancies.

947
01:59:04,362 --> 01:59:12,337
The nonpregnant reference values of 4.0 and 0.4 mU/L are represented as solid black lines.

948
01:59:12,337 --> 01:59:39,237
Upper shaded area represents the 28 percent of singleton pregnancies with TSH values above the 97.5th percentile threshold that would not have been identified as abnormal based on the assay reference value of 4.0 mU/L. Lower shaded area represents singleton pregnancies that would have been (falsely) identified as having TSH suppression based on the assay reference value of 0.4 mU/L.

949
01:59:39,237 --> 01:59:47,750
These complex alterations of thyroid regulation do not appear to alter maternal thyroid status as measured by metabolic studies.

950
01:59:47,750 --> 01:59:59,675
Although basal metabolic rate rises progressively by as much as 25 percent during normal pregnancy, most of this greater oxygen consumption can be attributed to fetal metabolic activity.

951
01:59:59,675 --> 02:00:09,325
If fetal body surface area is considered along with that of the mother, the predicted and observed basal metabolic rates are similar to those in nonpregnant women.

952
02:00:09,325 --> 02:00:17,237
Iodine Status As discussed earlier (p. 59), iodine requirements increase during normal pregnancy .

953
02:00:17,237 --> 02:00:24,687
In women with low or marginal intake, deficiency may manifest as low T4 and higher TSH levels.

954
02:00:24,687 --> 02:00:31,412
Importantly, more than one third of the world population lives in areas where iodine intake is marginal.

955
02:00:31,412 --> 02:00:36,325
Even in modernized countries, iodine deficiency is commonplace .

956
02:00:36,325 --> 02:00:41,925
For the fetus, early exposure to thyroid hormone is essential for the nervous system.

957
02:00:41,925 --> 02:00:51,400
Yet, despite public health programs to supplement iodine, severe deficiency that results in cretinism affects more than 2 million people globally .

958
02:00:51,400 --> 02:00:54,237
# Parathyroid Glands.

959
02:00:54,237 --> 02:01:03,750
During pregnancy, levels of calcitriol, which is the bioactive form of vitamin D, increase twofold and enhance intestinal calcium absorption .

960
02:01:03,750 --> 02:01:14,962
In an earlier longitudinal investigation of 20 women, all markers of bone turnover rose during normal pregnancy and failed to reach baseline levels by 12 months postpartum .

961
02:01:15,124 --> 02:01:23,124
Investigators concluded that the calcium needed for fetal growth and lactation may be drawn at least in part from the maternal skeleton.

962
02:01:23,074 --> 02:01:30,449
The factors affecting bone turnover create a net yield that favors fetal skeletal formation at the expense of the mother.

963
02:01:30,449 --> 02:01:35,574
As a result, pregnancy is a vulnerable period for osteoporosis .

964
02:01:35,574 --> 02:01:45,236
Parathyroid hormone (PTH) release is stimulated by acute or chronic declines in plasma calcium or acute drops in magnesium levels.

965
02:01:45,236 --> 02:01:50,724
Conversely, greater calcium and magnesium levels suppress PTH levels.

966
02:01:50,724 --> 02:02:02,349
The action of this hormone on bone resorption, intestinal absorption, and kidney reabsorption is to raise extracellular fluid calcium concentrations and lower phosphate levels.

967
02:02:02,349 --> 02:02:12,861
As discussed earlier (p. 59), fetal skeleton mineralization requires approximately 30 g of calcium, primarily during the third trimester.

968
02:02:12,861 --> 02:02:21,374
Although this amounts to only 3 percent of the total calcium held within the maternal skeleton, calcium demands still challenge the mother .

969
02:02:21,374 --> 02:02:26,211
Augmented maternal calcium absorption provides the additional calcium.

970
02:02:26,211 --> 02:02:35,574
During pregnancy, the amount of calcium absorbed rises gradually and reaches approximately 400 mg/d in the third trimester.

971
02:02:35,574 --> 02:02:43,886
Greater calcium absorption appears to be mediated by elevated maternal 1,25dihydroxyvitamin D concentrations.

972
02:02:43,886 --> 02:02:52,886
This occurs despite decreased PTH levels during early pregnancy, which is the normal stimulus for active vitamin D production within the kidney.

973
02:02:52,886 --> 02:03:01,261
Indeed, PTH plasma levels decline during the first trimester and then rise progressively throughout the remainder of pregnancy .

974
02:03:01,261 --> 02:03:11,036
The increased production of active vitamin D is likely due to placental production of either PTH or a PTH-related protein (PTH-rP).

975
02:03:11,036 --> 02:03:20,174
Outside pregnancy and lactation, PTH-rP is usually detectable only in serum of women with hypercalcemia due to malignancy.

976
02:03:20,174 --> 02:03:26,149
During pregnancy, however, PTH-rP concentrations rise significantly.

977
02:03:26,149 --> 02:03:31,111
This protein is synthesized in both fetal tissues and maternal breasts.

978
02:03:31,111 --> 02:03:38,549
Calcitonin is secreted by C cells that are located predominantly in the perifollicular areas of the thyroid gland.

979
02:03:38,549 --> 02:03:46,699
Calcitonin opposes actions of PTH and vitamin D and protects the maternal skeleton during times of calcium stress.

980
02:03:46,699 --> 02:03:52,474
As discussed, pregnancy and lactation cause profound maternal calcium stress.

981
02:03:52,474 --> 02:03:58,124
Indeed, fetal calcitonin levels are at least twofold higher than maternal levels .

982
02:03:58,124 --> 02:04:04,136
Although maternal calcitonin levels fall during pregnancy, they generally rise postpartum.

983
02:04:04,136 --> 02:04:09,574
Calcium and magnesium promote the biosynthesis and secretion of calcitonin.

984
02:04:09,574 --> 02:04:19,574
Various gastric hormones—gastrin, pentagastrin, glucagon, and pancreozymin—and food ingestion also promote calcitonin plasma levels.

985
02:04:19,574 --> 02:04:22,074
# Adrenal Glands.

986
02:04:22,074 --> 02:04:31,099
Cortisol In normal pregnancy, unlike their fetal counterparts, the maternal adrenal glands undergo little, if any, morphological change.

987
02:04:31,099 --> 02:04:39,436
The serum concentration of circulating cortisol rises, but much of it is bound by transcortin, the cortisol-binding globulin.

988
02:04:39,436 --> 02:04:47,561
The adrenal secretion rate of this principal glucocorticoid is not elevated, and it is probably lower than in the nonpregnant state.

989
02:04:47,561 --> 02:04:57,074
The metabolic clearance rate of cortisol, however, is diminished during pregnancy because its half-life is nearly doubled compared with that for nonpregnant women .

990
02:04:57,074 --> 02:05:07,299
Administration of estrogen, including most oral contraceptives, causes changes in serum cortisol levels and transcortin similar to those of pregnancy .

991
02:05:07,299 --> 02:05:17,249
During early pregnancy, the levels of circulating adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH ), also known as corticotropin, are dramatically reduced.

992
02:05:17,249 --> 02:05:23,699
As pregnancy progresses, ACTH and free cortisol levels rise equally and strikingly .

993
02:05:23,699 --> 02:05:27,574
This apparent paradox is not understood completely.

994
02:05:27,574 --> 02:05:35,849
Some suggest that greater free cortisol levels in pregnancy result from a “resetting” of the maternal feedback mechanism to higher thresholds .

995
02:05:35,849 --> 02:05:39,824
This might result from tissue refractoriness to cortisol.

996
02:05:39,824 --> 02:05:46,961
Others assert that these incongruities stem from an antagonistic action of progesterone on mineralocorticoids .

997
02:05:46,961 --> 02:05:55,011
Thus, in response to elevated progesterone levels during pregnancy, an elevated free cortisol is needed to maintain

998
02:05:55,180 --> 02:05:57,242
homeostasis.

999
02:05:57,192 --> 02:06:05,642
Other theories include possible roles for higher free cortisol in preparation for the stress of pregnancy, delivery, and lactation.

1000
02:06:05,642 --> 02:06:08,192
Figure 4-19.

1001
02:06:08,192 --> 02:06:17,655
Serial increases in serum cortisol (blue line) and adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) (red line) across normal pregnancy.

1002
02:06:17,655 --> 02:06:28,167
Aldosterone As early as 15 weeks’ gestation, the maternal adrenal glands secrete considerably higher amounts of aldosterone, the principal mineralocorticoid.

1003
02:06:28,167 --> 02:06:33,255
By the third trimester, approximately 1 mg/d is released.

1004
02:06:33,255 --> 02:06:39,030
If sodium intake is restricted, aldosterone secretion is even further elevated.

1005
02:06:39,030 --> 02:06:47,355
Concurrently, levels of renin and angiotensin II substrate normally rise, especially during the latter half of pregnancy.

1006
02:06:47,355 --> 02:06:59,280
This scenario promotes greater plasma levels of angiotensin II, which acts on the zona glomerulosa of the maternal adrenal glands and accounts for the markedly elevated aldosterone secretion.

1007
02:06:59,280 --> 02:07:09,967
Some suggest the increased aldosterone secretion during normal pregnancy affords protection against the natriuretic effect of progesterone and atrial natriuretic peptide.

1008
02:07:09,967 --> 02:07:19,567
Gennari-Moser and colleagues (2011) provide evidence that aldosterone and cortisol may modulate trophoblast growth and placental size.

1009
02:07:19,567 --> 02:07:27,467
Deoxycorticosterone Maternal plasma levels of this potent mineralocorticosteroid progressively increase during pregnancy.

1010
02:07:27,467 --> 02:07:36,905
Indeed, plasma levels of deoxycorticosterone rise to near 1500 pg/mL by term, a more than 15-fold increase .

1011
02:07:36,905 --> 02:07:46,005
This marked elevation does not derive from adrenal secretion but instead represents augmented kidney production resulting from estrogen stimulation.

1012
02:07:46,005 --> 02:07:58,105
The levels of deoxycorticosterone and its sulfate in fetal blood are appreciably higher than those in maternal blood, which suggests transfer of fetal deoxycorticosterone into the maternal compartment.

1013
02:07:58,105 --> 02:08:08,130
Androgens In balance, androgenic activity rises during pregnancy, and both maternal plasma levels of androstenedione and testosterone are increased.

1014
02:08:08,130 --> 02:08:13,567
This finding is not totally explained by alterations in their metabolic clearance.

1015
02:08:13,567 --> 02:08:19,755
Both androgens are converted to estradiol in the placenta, which increases their clearance rates.

1016
02:08:19,755 --> 02:08:27,280
Conversely, greater plasma sex hormone–binding globulin levels in gravidas retard testosterone clearance.

1017
02:08:27,280 --> 02:08:34,467
Thus, the production rates of maternal testosterone and androstenedione during human pregnancy are increased.

1018
02:08:34,467 --> 02:08:41,092
The source of this higher C19-steroid production is unknown, but it likely originates in the ovary.

1019
02:08:41,092 --> 02:08:48,517
Interestingly, little or no testosterone in maternal plasma enters the fetal circulation as testosterone.

1020
02:08:48,517 --> 02:09:01,242
Even when massive testosterone levels are found in the circulation of pregnant women, as with androgen-secreting tumors, testosterone concentrations in umbilical cord blood are likely to be undetectable.

1021
02:09:01,242 --> 02:09:07,892
This results from the near complete trophoblastic conversion of testosterone to 17β-estradiol.

1022
02:09:07,892 --> 02:09:14,630
Maternal serum and urine levels of dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate are lower during normal pregnancy.

1023
02:09:14,630 --> 02:09:23,667
This stems from a greater metabolic clearance through extensive maternal hepatic 16α-hydroxylation and placental conversion to estrogen .

1024
02:09:23,667 --> 02:09:26,167
Musculoskeletal System.

1025
02:09:26,167 --> 02:09:30,080
Progressive lordosis is characteristic of pregnancy.

1026
02:09:30,080 --> 02:09:39,280
Compensating for the anterior position of the enlarging uterus, lordosis shifts the center of gravity dorsally and over the lower extremities.

1027
02:09:39,280 --> 02:09:45,642
The sacroiliac, sacrococcygeal, and pubic joints have greater mobility during pregnancy.

1028
02:09:45,642 --> 02:09:58,117
However, as discussed earlier, increased joint laxity and associated discomfort during pregnancy do not correlate with increased maternal serum levels of estradiol, progesterone, or relaxin.

1029
02:09:58,117 --> 02:10:02,480
Most relaxation takes place in the first half of pregnancy.

1030
02:10:02,480 --> 02:10:08,455
It may contribute to maternal posture alterations and in turn create lower back discomfort.

1031
02:10:08,455 --> 02:10:20,655
As discussed in Chapter 36 (p. 644), although some symphyseal separation likely accompanies many deliveries, those greater than 1 cm may cause significant pain .

1032
02:10:20,655 --> 02:10:26,755
Aching, numbness, and weakness also occasionally are experienced in the upper extremities.

1033
02:10:26,755 --> 02:10:33,567
This may result from the marked lordosis and associated anterior neck flexion and shoulder girdle slumping.

1034
02:10:33,567 --> 02:10:35,117
These

1035
02:10:35,284 --> 02:10:39,084
can produce traction on the ulnar and median nerves.

1036
02:10:39,034 --> 02:10:43,859
The latter may give rise to symptoms mistaken for the carpal tunnel syndrome .

1037
02:10:43,859 --> 02:10:50,534
Joint strengthening begins immediately following delivery and is usually complete within 3 to 5 months.

1038
02:10:50,534 --> 02:10:58,609
Pelvic dimensions measured by MR imaging up to 3 months postpartum are not significantly different from prepregnancy values .

1039
02:10:58,609 --> 02:11:00,846
Central Nervous System.

1040
02:11:00,846 --> 02:11:06,721
Changes to the central nervous system during pregnancy are relatively few and mostly subtle.

1041
02:11:06,721 --> 02:11:14,271
Zeeman and coworkers (2003) used MR imaging to measure cerebral blood flow across pregnancy.

1042
02:11:14,271 --> 02:11:29,621
They found that mean blood flow in the middle and posterior cerebral arteries declined progressively from 147 and 56 mL/min when nonpregnant to 118 and 44 mL/min late in pregnancy, respectively.

1043
02:11:29,621 --> 02:11:34,934
Others have found similar middle cerebral artery blood flow changes with ultrasound .

1044
02:11:34,934 --> 02:11:38,884
Mechanisms and significance of the decline are unknown.

1045
02:11:38,884 --> 02:11:44,109
It appears that pregnancy does not affect cerebrovascular autoregulation .

1046
02:11:44,109 --> 02:11:46,159
# Memory.

1047
02:11:46,159 --> 02:11:53,596
Women often report problems with attention, concentration, and memory throughout pregnancy and the early puerperium.

1048
02:11:53,596 --> 02:11:59,721
Systematic studies of memory in pregnancy, however, are limited and often anecdotal.

1049
02:11:59,721 --> 02:12:08,771
In a metaanalysis, Davies and coworkers (2018) reported pregnancy-related memory decline was worst in the third trimester.

1050
02:12:08,771 --> 02:12:17,334
This decline was not attributable to depression, anxiety, sleep deprivation, or other physical changes associated with pregnancy.

1051
02:12:17,334 --> 02:12:21,259
It was transient and quickly resolved following delivery.

1052
02:12:21,259 --> 02:12:32,434
In follow up studies, preeclampsia and eclampsia were found to lead to both short- and long-term cognitive disability (Aukes, 2007; Dayan, 2018).

1053
02:12:32,434 --> 02:12:34,434
# Eyes.

1054
02:12:34,434 --> 02:12:40,984
Intraocular pressure declines during pregnancy and is attributed partly to greater vitreous outflow.

1055
02:12:40,984 --> 02:12:46,446
Corneal sensitivity is decreased, and the greatest changes are late in gestation.

1056
02:12:46,446 --> 02:12:53,559
Most pregnant women demonstrate a measurable but slight increase in corneal thickness, thought to be due to edema.

1057
02:12:53,559 --> 02:12:59,284
Consequently, they may have difficulty with previously comfortable contact lenses.

1058
02:12:59,284 --> 02:13:09,021
Brownish-red opacities on the posterior surface of the cornea —Krukenberg spindles—are observed with a higher than expected frequency during pregnancy.

1059
02:13:09,021 --> 02:13:15,721
Hormonal effects similar to those observed for skin lesions are believed to cause this increased pigmentation.

1060
02:13:15,721 --> 02:13:24,246
Other than transient loss of accommodation reported during both pregnancy and lactation, visual function is unaffected by pregnancy.

1061
02:13:24,246 --> 02:13:32,096
These changes during pregnancy and pathological eye aberrations were reviewed by Gilbert and colleagues (2019).

1062
02:13:32,096 --> 02:13:34,109
# Sleep.

1063
02:13:34,109 --> 02:13:47,296
Beginning as early as 12 weeks’ gestation and extending through the first 2 months postpartum, women have difficulty with falling asleep, frequent awakenings, fewer hours of night sleep, and reduced sleep efficiency.

1064
02:13:47,296 --> 02:13:58,434
These symptoms are part of the spectrum of sleep-disordered breathing, the most severe of which is obstructive sleep apnea (Ayyar, 2018; Dominguez, 2018).

1065
02:13:58,434 --> 02:14:07,121
The greatest disruption of sleep is encountered postpartum and may contribute to postpartum blues or to frank depression and suicidal ideation .

